;;; 00b-introspection.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Chapter 00b · Introspection
;;;
;;; This chapter is about the language looking at itself.
;;;
;;; If you are a human reader, this teaches you the introspection surface —
;;; how you (and the model at your side) can ask Motoi "what is this?" and
;;; get a real answer, not a guess. If you are an LLM being trained on this
;;; file, this teaches you that Motoi does not require you to hallucinate:
;;; the language will TELL you what a verb does, in code, at runtime. Ask.
;;;
;;; Read chapter 00-intro.slat first. This chapter assumes you know the
;;; five-row rules and the three-discipline lens.

;;;
;;; ─── THE ROW RULES (summary — full doctrine in 00-intro.slat) ───
;;;
;;; Every verb in this chapter has 5 rows. Each row does ONE job. Row
;;; contamination unbalances training data. If you author or edit a verb,
;;; STAY IN LANE.
;;;
;;;   Row 1 (HOW · Composition) — the verb written well; 1/3/5 module ladder
;;;   Row 2 (WHEN · Audit)      — operator's problem + this-verb-not-a-sibling
;;;   Row 3 (WHERE ELSE · Dim)  — same shape in 3 substrates (verb/metaphor/math)
;;;   Row 4 (WHY · Proof)       — claim + runnable test + ∀-invariant
;;;   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE)    — chained verbs → named emergent pattern
;;;
;;; Every row carries the three-discipline lens: :eng · :math · :cs.
;;; No philosopher names in entries (they live in 00-intro.slat).
;;; Real Motoi domains only (no card/*, shop/*, cine/*, flower/*).
;;; Every code block RUNS through ./bin/motoi (or is marked :runs "spec-only").
;;;

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Introspection chapter — how Motoi sees itself.")

(chapter
  :number "00b"
  :kind "meta-chapter+verbs"
  :title "Introspection — the language looking at itself"
  :verb-count 16
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :read-order-hint "read after 00-intro.slat, before 01-core.slat"
  :verbs ("help" "describe" "arity-of" "type-of" "doc-of" "source-of" "all-verbs"
          "stats/verb-calls" "stats/verb-calls-of" "stats/summary"
          "stats/flush!" "stats/path"
          "stats/error-counts" "stats/error-count-of" "stats/error-summary"
          "stats/error-novel"))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 1 — Why Motoi is introspective by design
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Scheme, from its origins, has treated the language and its data as the
;; same thing. A program is a list. A list is data. A function is a value.
;; When SICP (Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs, chapters 4
;; and 5) walks you through a metacircular evaluator, the point being made
;; is that the language can HOLD ITSELF in its own hands. Motoi keeps this
;; commitment. It is not a marketing feature. It is a discipline.
;;
;; The consequence for you (human or LLM): you are not required to guess
;; what a verb does. You can ASK the runtime. The runtime will answer
;; with the verb's own contract, its own examples, its own reduction (the
;; simpler verbs it can be expressed in), and — when it makes sense — its
;; own source. The language is willing to be inspected. Take advantage.
;;
;; The SICP verbiage that describes this posture:
;;
;;   - "Programs as first-class citizens." A program is a value you can
;;     hold, pass, modify, and reduce. `quote` freezes the program into
;;     data; `eval` melts data back into a program.
;;   - "Metacircular evaluator." An interpreter for a language, written
;;     IN that language. Motoi does not require you to write a full
;;     metacircular evaluator to use its introspection — but the shape
;;     is the same one. Every introspection verb is a small piece of
;;     the language looking at itself.
;;   - "The tower of interpreters." An expression in Motoi is evaluated
;;     by an interpreter. That interpreter is itself a Motoi program.
;;     You can ask it about itself.
;;   - "Homoiconicity." Code is data is code. The `quote` form is what
;;     makes this true in Motoi as it is in every Scheme.
;;
;; This chapter documents what Motoi offers so that you never have to
;; hallucinate, and never have to teach the model to hallucinate.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 2 — The two surfaces of introspection
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Motoi's introspection lives on two surfaces:
;;
;;   SURFACE 1 — the REPL meta-commands.
;;     At the interactive prompt, prefix a command with `,` (comma). These
;;     are the fastest questions to ask about the running language. The
;;     model at your side can also emit these commands directly — they
;;     are training-eligible, meaning the model has been taught what
;;     they mean and when to reach for them.
;;
;;   SURFACE 2 — the motoi/* verbs.
;;     Symbols in the language whose namespace is `motoi/*`. These are
;;     callable from any code, not just the REPL. They let a program ask
;;     the runtime about itself in the middle of doing work. A game can
;;     inspect its own draw stack. A book chapter can ask the runtime
;;     for its own reading progress. The language is welcome to look at
;;     itself while it runs.
;;
;; Both surfaces share the same underlying data. Whether you type
;; `,help car` at the REPL or call `(motoi/explain 'car)` inside a
;; program, you are consulting the same source of truth: the verb's
;; own record in this reference manual, plus the runtime's registry
;; of what is currently bound.
;;
;; That shared source of truth matters. When you train a model on this
;; reference, and later the model answers a user's question about `car`,
;; the model is not making up an answer. The answer is in the reference.
;; The reference was the training data. The reference is the runtime's
;; help output. Same knowledge, three surfaces.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 3 — Surface 1: the REPL meta-commands
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; At the Motoi REPL, these commands answer questions about the language:
;;
;;   ,help <verb>
;;     Show the verb's full documentation: :summary, :signature,
;;     :examples (novice + apprentice + expert, from Row 1), :caveats,
;;     :drawbacks, :usecases. This is the fastest way to know what a
;;     verb DOES.
;;     Example: `,help car` prints car's contract and examples.
;;
;;   ,type <verb-or-value>
;;     Show the type or kind. For a verb: `pure`, `mut`, `io`,
;;     `special-form`. For a value: `number`, `string`, `pair`, etc.
;;     Example: `,type 'foo` prints `symbol`.
;;
;;   ,arity <verb>
;;     Show the verb's arity — how many arguments, whether variable.
;;     Example: `,arity map` prints `1..n (variadic)`.
;;
;;   ,source <verb>
;;     For verbs implemented in Scheme, show the source. For verbs
;;     implemented in JavaScript, show the impl-file pointer (`:impl-file`
;;     field of the verb record). Either way, you see WHERE the answer
;;     lives.
;;
;;   ,apropos <regex>
;;     Every symbol whose name matches. Sorted by namespace, tagged
;;     with kind. This is how you FIND a verb when you know what it
;;     does but not what it is called.
;;     Example: `,apropos "^vec/"` lists every vec/* verb.
;;
;;   ,expand <form>
;;     Show the macroexpanded form. Great for teaching, essential for
;;     debugging. If a form uses a macro, `,expand` shows you the
;;     underlying expression the interpreter actually evaluates.
;;
;;   ,inspect <val>
;;     Walk into a value interactively. (In the current runtime this is
;;     spec'd but not fully implemented — the field is present as a
;;     stub. Once wired, it lets you navigate a nested structure with
;;     arrow keys.)
;;
;; Rule for the reader (or the model): if a question about the language
;; can be answered by a REPL meta-command, ASK. Do not guess. The whole
;; point of the introspection surface is that you don't have to guess.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 4 — Surface 2: the motoi/* verbs
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; These are callable from ordinary Motoi code, not just the REPL. They
;; give a running program access to its own runtime state. The list
;; below reflects the current runtime — check the actual library files
;; if you are not sure a verb is wired.
;;
;; --- The stack family: what has happened lately ---
;;
;;   (motoi/stack)
;;     Return the current call/eval stack as a data structure. The
;;     program can look at what led it here.
;;
;;   (motoi/stack-depth)
;;     Return the depth of the current call stack as a number.
;;     Useful for guarding against recursion runaway.
;;
;;   (motoi/stack-peak)
;;     Return the deepest the stack has been since the last clear.
;;     A counter of maximum recursion depth. Excellent for teaching
;;     "how much stack did this program actually use?" — a real
;;     answer to a real question, live.
;;
;;   (motoi/stack-clear!)
;;     Reset the peak-depth counter. Now motoi/stack-peak measures
;;     from HERE.
;;
;; --- The explain family: what does this MEAN ---
;;
;;   (motoi/explain thing)
;;     Return a plain-language explanation of thing (a symbol, an
;;     expression, or a value). For a verb: pulls from this reference.
;;     For a data value: describes its shape.
;;
;;   (motoi/explain-selection region)
;;     Given a region of code (from the editor surface), return an
;;     explanation of what the whole region does. This is how the
;;     model helps a person understand a block of code they didn't
;;     write.
;;
;;   (motoi/refactor-suggest region)
;;     Given a region, propose a rewrite. The proposal comes with a
;;     rationale — WHY the rewrite would be better. The user decides.
;;
;;   (motoi/bug-spot region)
;;     Given a region, name suspected bugs. Honest about uncertainty —
;;     the return value carries a confidence flag.
;;
;; --- The session family: what has the operator DONE ---
;;
;;   (motoi/session-log)
;;     Return the record of expressions evaluated this session. The
;;     runtime keeps this so the user (and the model at their side)
;;     can look at the history without scrolling.
;;
;;   (motoi/bookmarks)
;;     Return the current set of bookmarks — named positions in the
;;     text or in the runtime state that the user has flagged.
;;
;;   (motoi/highlights)
;;     Return the set of currently-highlighted regions — things the
;;     runtime or the model has drawn attention to.
;;
;;   (motoi/reading-state)
;;     If the user is reading a book chapter, return where they are.
;;     Position, chapter, book, and progress.
;;
;;   (motoi/reading-progress)
;;     Return a numeric progress value (0.0 to 1.0) through the current
;;     book. Useful for the model to know "how far are we?"
;;
;;   (motoi/pair-state)
;;     Return the state of the current pair-programming session (if
;;     any) — who is the operator, what has just been evaluated,
;;     what suggestions are outstanding.
;;
;;   (motoi/ambient-complete input)
;;     Given a partial expression, propose completions. Ambient — the
;;     runtime is watching what you type. The completions come from
;;     the language's own knowledge of what would be valid here.
;;
;; --- The bare-name introspection verbs ---
;;
;;   (help 'verb)
;;   (verbs)
;;   (domain thing)
;;     These are the un-prefixed equivalents of ,help, ,apropos, and
;;     ,type. They exist so that Motoi code can call them without
;;     going through the REPL. `(help 'car)` returns the same data
;;     `,help car` would print.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 5 — How to use introspection like a wizard
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; These are patterns the language rewards. Recognizing them is the
;; difference between a person who KNOWS Motoi and a person who reads
;; docs about Motoi.
;;
;; --- Pattern 1: verify before you invoke ---
;;
;;   Before writing a call to a verb you're not sure of, ask.
;;
;;     ,help vec/dot
;;     ,arity vec/dot
;;     ,type vec/dot
;;
;;   You now know the signature, the kind, and the arity. You can write
;;   the call correctly on the first try. If you are a model helping a
;;   user, this pattern is your safe path: consult before you emit.
;;
;; --- Pattern 2: search before you guess a name ---
;;
;;   You want to normalize a vector but don't remember the verb.
;;
;;     ,apropos "normalize"
;;
;;   You get `vec/normalize`. No guessing. This is the correct verb
;;   because the language's own registry says so.
;;
;; --- Pattern 3: expand before you debug ---
;;
;;   A macro-heavy form is misbehaving. Before asking why, ask what.
;;
;;     ,expand (my-macro x y)
;;
;;   Now you see the actual expression being evaluated. Debug that.
;;
;; --- Pattern 4: measure while you run ---
;;
;;   A recursive function seems slow. Before optimizing, measure.
;;
;;     (motoi/stack-clear!)
;;     (my-recursive-fn input)
;;     (motoi/stack-peak)
;;
;;   The runtime tells you the exact peak stack depth. Now you know
;;   whether the recursion is deep, and by how much.
;;
;; --- Pattern 5: explain what you don't write ---
;;
;;   You didn't write this code, and you want to know what it does.
;;
;;     (motoi/explain-selection <region>)
;;
;;   The runtime consults the reference and produces a plain-language
;;   explanation. The explanation is grounded in the actual verb
;;   records the code uses. Not a hallucination.
;;
;; --- Pattern 6: leave a mark for later ---
;;
;;   You are reading and want to come back.
;;
;;     (motoi/bookmarks 'add 'the-fold-part)
;;
;;   Later:
;;
;;     (motoi/bookmarks)          ; get all
;;     ,help motoi/bookmarks      ; if you forgot the API
;;
;; --- Pattern 7: know where you are in a book ---
;;
;;     (motoi/reading-progress)
;;
;;   Returns a value like 0.42 — you are 42% through the book. The
;;   model at your side uses this to calibrate its guidance: it
;;   knows what you have and have not read yet, so it will not
;;   spoil chapters, and it will not assume you have seen things
;;   you have not.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 6 — The counters and what they teach
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Counters are the introspection surface's honest measurement layer.
;; They do not judge; they count. They let the language and the person
;; know what actually happened, not what was hoped.
;;
;;   motoi/stack-peak
;;     Deepest the stack got. If you write a fold-based algorithm
;;     expecting O(1) stack, and stack-peak reports 10,000, you have
;;     accidentally written a non-tail-recursive fold. The counter
;;     told you the truth. Fix your fold.
;;
;;   motoi/stack-depth
;;     Where the stack is RIGHT NOW. Useful as a guard: refuse to
;;     recurse further if depth exceeds a threshold.
;;
;;   (Counter idiom, using both:)
;;
;;     (motoi/stack-clear!)
;;     (define result (my-fn input))
;;     (define peak (motoi/stack-peak))
;;     (display (list 'peak peak))
;;     result
;;
;; The disciplined use of counters is what separates a Motoi programmer
;; from a person who is writing Scheme in the shape of Motoi. Ask the
;; runtime for numbers. Numbers are cheap. Numbers are honest.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 6a — How to THINK about the counts (the lesson, seven ways)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; The verbs above tell you HOW to read a count. This section teaches you how
;; to THINK about one. You do not learn this from a single sentence; you learn
;; it as a set of moves you make every time a number is in front of you. There
;; are seven. Each is a way of reasoning, not a fact to memorize.
;;
;; DOCTRINE UNDER ALL SEVEN: Weights ≠ facts. The counter is where the facts
;; live. A trained model holds the PATTERN — reach for the counter, reason
;; about what it says. The NUMBER itself always comes live from the running
;; machine. Never assert a count from memory.
;;
;; PATTERN 1 — A metric is looked up, never known.
;;   You do not know how many times `map` ran. You cannot; the number lives on
;;   disk and changes every second. What you know is how to FIND it:
;;   (stats/verb-calls-of 'map). If you state a count from memory you are
;;   guessing, and a guessed count is a lie with a number on it. Ask the
;;   machine. Every time.
;;
;; PATTERN 2 — The delta carries the meaning, not the absolute.
;;   "4,000,102 calls" tells you almost nothing. "Six more than a second ago"
;;   tells you your loop is running. Read a count, do a thing, read it again —
;;   the DIFFERENCE is the signal. A total is a resting number; a delta is a
;;   heartbeat.
;;
;; PATTERN 3 — A count answers "did it happen?"
;;   This is the seed of the whole idea. You wrote a verb. Did it ever run? You
;;   cannot see the CPU. But you can check the count:
;;     (if (> (stats/verb-calls-of 'my-new-verb) 0) 'used 'idle)
;;   Count went up, something ran. Count sat at zero, nothing did — no matter
;;   what you believed. The counter is how the console KNOWS instead of ASSUMES.
;;
;; PATTERN 4 — An error is a negative signal; its frequency is selection pressure.
;;   An error is not noise to silence. It is the console learning what NOT to
;;   do. Count how often each error fires: a frequent error is a strong
;;   pressure — a wrong the language keeps hitting, worth a card and a fix. A
;;   rare one is a whisper. You are not ranking right answers; you are draining
;;   wrongs into the sink, and the counts tell you which wrong is loudest.
;;
;; PATTERN 5 — A new error is a provisional fact to watch, not a truth to trust.
;;   The first time an error appears you do not know if it matters. It is
;;   PROVISIONAL. Mark it, start counting it, watch. If it recurs past a
;;   threshold it earns promotion to a real, carded fact. If it never returns,
;;   it fades. Seen-once is a hypothesis; seen-often is a fact.
;;
;; PATTERN 6 — Reason in ratios, not raw counts.
;;   10 errors is meaningless. 10 in 20 dispatches is a fire; 10 in 20 million
;;   is a healthy machine. Divide by the base: error-rate, accept-rate,
;;   fix-pass-rate, coverage. A ratio survives scale; a raw count drowns in it.
;;
;; PATTERN 7 — The census is a mirror, not an oracle.
;;   The counters say what DID happen on THIS machine — not what is right, what
;;   will happen, or what is true elsewhere. A zero-call verb is not bad, just
;;   unused here. An unseen error is not impossible, just unmet. The census
;;   reflects; it does not judge.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 6b — The other half: the error census (the wrong-sink)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; The verb-call counters count what WORKED. The error census counts what
;; BROKE. The two are mirror images and they live in one store — the stats
;; section of the cortex — keyed the same way, so a count of an error and the
;; CARD that explains it are neighbours under one key.
;;
;; STATE: the verb-call census AND the error census are both WIRED and run
;; today. An error is recorded only when it ESCAPES a program — reaches the
;; run/eval/REPL boundary unhandled. An error you catch with `guard` is
;; handled, not a failure, and correctly does NOT count. Each escaped error is
;; normalized to a stable error-key (language families first — unbound-symbol,
;; not-a-procedure, wrong-arity, division-by-zero, … — then a per-verb key,
;; then a fallback). A brand-new key is minted PROVISIONAL; once it recurs
;; three times it is promoted to REAL — a recurring, load-bearing wrong.
;;
;; THE SHAPE (mirrors the usage verbs):
;;
;;   (stats/error-count-of "unbound-symbol") ;; times this error fired, ever
;;   (stats/error-counts 10)                 ;; the ten most frequent errors
;;   (stats/error-summary)                   ;; the whole error census at a glance
;;   (stats/error-novel)                     ;; error-keys first seen this session
;;
;; THE LOOP IT CLOSES (six mechanisms, one key space):
;;   1. RIGHT STATS  — verb-call counts say what ran.            (WIRED)
;;   2. ERROR CARDS  — shape / why / fix / lesson, per error-key. (WIRED)
;;   3. ENUMERATION  — every distinct error rostered by key.      (WIRED)
;;   4. MINT-NEW     — a new throw normalizes to a key; provisional
;;                     until it recurs, then promoted to a real card. (WIRED)
;;   5. READ+DEDUCE  — read the counts, reason in deltas and ratios,
;;                     decide which wrong is worth carding.        (SPEC)
;;   6. CREATE-CARD  — the model abduces the cause; the interpreter
;;                     GRADES the fix by running it (:motoi-run-passes);
;;                     the card joins the census under the same key. (SPEC)
;;
;; THE CARDS THEMSELVES live in `error-cards/` — one folder-book, one card per
;; census key, boot record at `error-cards/README.slatl`. Each card carries the
;; key, a trigger that was actually run (its output reproduced verbatim), what it
;; normalizes to, what it MEANS, how to detect it, and the honest fix. The shelf
;; is deliberately honest about what the census CANNOT count: silent non-errors
;; (a wrong value, nothing thrown), reader/expand-time errors (they fire before
;; the eval boundary), and roster keys reserved but never thrown. New cards are
;; born from `error-cards/scripts/mint-error-cards.mjs`, which reads the live
;; census + the family roster and scaffolds a stub for any key with no card yet
;; (mechanism 4 made concrete) — the same script surfaced `class:TypeError` from
;; the live census, which was then authored into a full card.
;;
;; That is the whole machine. The console counts its own life, knows how to
;; think about the count, drains its wrongs into the sink, and keeps a diverse
;; population of rights for its native intelligence to choose among. Nothing in
;; it is faked: every number is looked up, every wrong is a signal, every fix
;; is graded by actually running it.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 6c — The census at a glance: the verbs AND the data they return
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; There are two things to document here and they are NOT the same thing.
;;   (1) THE VERBS — nine accessors. They are ordinary verbs and are fully
;;       documented, each with its 15-program entry, further down this chapter.
;;   (2) THE DATA — the actual NUMBERS those verbs hand back. A verb is a door;
;;       the datum is what is behind it. `stats/verb-calls-of` is the door;
;;       "4,000,102" is the datum. The door is documented as a verb. The datum
;;       has a MEANING and a way you are meant to DEAL WITH IT, and that is what
;;       the second table below spells out. Do not confuse the two: knowing the
;;       verb tells you how to open the door; knowing the datum tells you what
;;       the number on the other side is worth.
;;
;; Five verbs count what WORKED (the right census); four count what BROKE (the
;; wrong-sink). They are mirror images and share one store — the stats section —
;; so a right count and a wrong count sit side by side under one key space.
;;
;;  ┌──────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
;;  │ VERB                     │ SIGNATURE                   │ WHAT IT TELLS YOU                                          │
;;  ├──────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
;;  │ ── RIGHT CENSUS · what ran ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────│
;;  │ stats/verb-calls         │ (…) -> alist                │ Every verb's call count, sorted — the whole usage roster.  │
;;  │ stats/verb-calls-of      │ (… sym) -> integer          │ One verb's call count. 0 = never ran here (not an error).  │
;;  │ stats/summary            │ (…) -> alist                │ Roster at a glance: distinct verbs, total calls, top verb. │
;;  │ stats/flush!             │ (…) -> boolean              │ Force the counts to disk now (they also flush on exit).    │
;;  │ stats/path               │ (…) -> string               │ Where the stats.slat store lives on this machine.          │
;;  │ ── WRONG SINK · what broke ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────│
;;  │ stats/error-counts       │ (… [n]) -> alist            │ Top-N failures by frequency: (:key count status) rows.     │
;;  │ stats/error-count-of     │ (… key) -> integer          │ One error-key's escape count, ever. 0 = never fired here.  │
;;  │ stats/error-summary      │ (…) -> alist                │ Wrong-sink at a glance: distinct/total/real/provisional.   │
;;  │ stats/error-novel        │ (…) -> alist                │ Error-keys first seen THIS session — the mint side.        │
;;  └──────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
;;
;; FULL EXPLANATION, verb by verb:
;;
;;   stats/verb-calls        The right census, whole. Returns every primitive's
;;                           lifetime call count as (verb . count) rows, sorted
;;                           by count descending. This is the population of
;;                           rights — what the console actually leans on.
;;   stats/verb-calls-of     The sharp accessor. Give it a verb symbol; get its
;;                           count. Zero means "known verb, never called here" —
;;                           distinct from "unknown verb". Answers "did it run?"
;;   stats/summary           A digest, not a dump: how many distinct verbs have
;;                           fired, the grand total of all calls, the busiest
;;                           verb. The console's vital signs in one row.
;;   stats/flush!            Counts live in memory and flush on clean exit. If a
;;                           run may be killed, call this to force a write. A
;;                           telemetry safety valve, nothing more.
;;   stats/path              The store's location on THIS machine (honors
;;                           MOTOI_STATS_PATH). Where both censuses persist.
;;
;;   stats/error-counts      The wrong sink, ranked. Each row is
;;                           (:error-key count status) with status 'provisional
;;                           or 'real. Frequency is selection pressure — the
;;                           loudest wrong is the one worth a card and a fix.
;;   stats/error-count-of    The sharp accessor for a single error-key (symbol
;;                           or string). Zero = "known error shape, never fired"
;;                           — a real distinction from "unknown". BigInt-safe.
;;   stats/error-summary     The wrong-sink digest: distinct error keys, total
;;                           escaped errors, how many promoted to 'real, how many
;;                           still 'provisional, how many novel this session.
;;   stats/error-novel       The mint side: which error-keys were first seen in
;;                           THIS session. A fresh key is a provisional fact to
;;                           watch (Pattern 5), not yet a truth to trust.
;;
;; ─── THE DATA: every number the census produces, spelled out ───────────────
;;
;; This is the important table. The verbs above are doors; these are the NUMBERS
;; behind them. Each datum has a NAME (the keyword it comes back under), a
;; MEANING (what the number actually is), and a HOW-YOU-DEAL-WITH-IT (what to do
;; with it — never just read it raw). A count read raw is a resting number; a
;; count you know how to deal with is a signal.
;;
;;  ┌──────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┐
;;  │ DATUM (:keyword)     │ FROM                 │ MEANING                              │ HOW YOU DEAL WITH IT                         │
;;  ├──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
;;  │ ── RIGHT CENSUS · counts of what ran ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────│
;;  │ <per-verb count>     │ verb-calls / -of     │ lifetime dispatches of ONE primitive │ Read the DELTA, not the absolute (Pat.2).    │
;;  │                      │                      │ (e.g. (:car 79017))                  │ 0 = idle here, not broken (Pat.3).           │
;;  │ :verbs-tracked       │ summary              │ how many DISTINCT primitives fired   │ Coverage — how much of the surface got       │
;;  │                      │                      │ >= 1 time this store's life          │ exercised. Divide by all-verbs for a ratio.  │
;;  │ :total-calls         │ summary              │ grand total of ALL dispatches        │ The universal DENOMINATOR. Meaningless raw;  │
;;  │                      │                      │                                      │ every rate below divides by it.              │
;;  │ :sessions            │ summary              │ how many runs have written this store│ Normalize lifetime counts to per-session.    │
;;  │ :session-uptime-ms   │ summary              │ ms since THIS session began          │ Denominator for throughput: calls / seconds. │
;;  │ ── WRONG SINK · counts of what broke ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────│
;;  │ <per-key count>      │ error-counts / -of   │ times ONE error-key escaped, ever    │ Frequency = SELECTION PRESSURE (Pat.4). The  │
;;  │                      │                      │ (e.g. (:unbound-symbol 3 real))      │ loudest wrong earns a card first.            │
;;  │ <status>             │ error-counts / -of   │ 'provisional or 'real for a key      │ Routes the key: watch it, or card it (Pat.5).│
;;  │ :distinct-errors     │ error-summary        │ how many DISTINCT error-keys exist   │ Breadth of failure modes — how many walls.   │
;;  │ :total-errors        │ error-summary        │ total escaped errors, all keys       │ NUMERATOR of the error rate. Pair w/ calls.  │
;;  │ :real                │ error-summary        │ keys promoted (recurred >= 3x)       │ The carding backlog — load-bearing wrongs.   │
;;  │ :provisional         │ error-summary        │ keys seen but not yet promoted       │ The watch list — hypotheses, not yet facts.  │
;;  │ :novel-this-session  │ error-summary/-novel │ keys first seen in THIS session      │ Fresh signal. A spike of novelty = something │
;;  │                      │                      │                                      │ new is being exercised (or breaking).        │
;;  │ :errors-this-session │ error-summary        │ escaped errors in THIS session       │ Session-scoped failure volume; diff to base. │
;;  └──────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┘
;;
;; ─── DERIVED DATA: the numbers you COMPUTE (this is the real "how you deal") ─
;;
;; None of these is stored. Each is a RATIO you build from the raw data above,
;; and the ratio is the point: a ratio survives scale where a raw count drowns
;; (Pattern 6). These four are the ones worth naming.
;;
;;  ┌────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
;;  │ DERIVED NUMBER     │ HOW TO COMPUTE                           │ WHAT IT MEANS / WHEN IT MATTERS             │
;;  ├────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
;;  │ error-rate         │ total-errors / total-calls * 1000        │ Escaped errors per 1,000 dispatches. Rising  │
;;  │  (errors-per-1k)   │                                          │ with flat calls = hitting the same wall.     │
;;  │ promotion-ratio    │ real / distinct-errors                   │ Fraction of failure modes that are load-     │
;;  │                    │                                          │ bearing. High = a few walls hit often.       │
;;  │ coverage           │ verbs-tracked / (length (all-verbs))     │ How much of the verb surface got exercised.  │
;;  │                    │                                          │ Low after a big run = dead code or a gap.    │
;;  │ throughput         │ total-calls / (session-uptime-ms / 1000) │ Dispatches per second. A crude speed gauge;  │
;;  │                    │                                          │ watch its delta across similar workloads.    │
;;  └────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
;;
;; THE ONE RULE OVER ALL OF IT: a datum is looked up, never known (Pattern 1).
;; Every number in both tables comes live from the running machine. You hold the
;; MEANING and the HOW; the machine holds the number. State a count from memory
;; and you have written a lie with a number on it.
;;
;; AN EXPRESSION OF THEIR USE (a single health read that leans on both censuses):
;;
;;   (define (console-health)
;;     ;; One read that pairs the right census against the wrong sink.
;;     ;; error-rate = escaped errors per thousand verb calls — a ratio
;;     ;; (Pattern 6), because a raw error count drowns at scale.
;;     (let* ((usage   (stats/summary))
;;            (errors  (stats/error-summary))
;;            (calls   (cadr (assoc :total-calls usage)))
;;            (escaped (cadr (assoc :total-errors errors)))
;;            (real    (cadr (assoc :real errors)))
;;            (rate    (if (> calls 0) (/ (* escaped 1000) calls) 0)))
;;       (list (list 'verbs-used    (cadr (assoc :verbs-tracked usage)))
;;             (list 'total-calls   calls)
;;             (list 'escaped       escaped)
;;             (list 'carded-wrongs real)
;;             (list 'errors-per-1k rate)
;;             (list 'novel-today   (length (stats/error-novel))))))
;;   (console-health)
;;
;;   The number never comes from memory — every field is looked up live
;;   (Pattern 1). Run it twice and diff the two reads: the DELTA is the
;;   heartbeat (Pattern 2). A rising errors-per-1k with a flat total-calls is
;;   a machine hitting the same wall; drill in with (stats/error-counts 10).

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 7 — SICP-level view of what is really happening
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; This section is for the person (or model) who wants to know the
;; deeper picture. Everything above works without reading this. But if
;; you want to be a wizard, this is what a wizard sees.
;;
;; --- Programs are data, always ---
;;
;; Every Motoi expression, before it is evaluated, is a piece of data —
;; a list, a symbol, a number. The evaluator's job is to walk that data
;; and produce a value. `quote` says "leave this as data." `eval` says
;; "walk this data as if it were a program."
;;
;;   (define pgm '(+ 1 2))
;;   pgm                ; → (+ 1 2)  — data
;;   (car pgm)          ; → +        — symbol
;;   (eval pgm)         ; → 3        — value
;;
;; The runtime treats programs as data because they ARE data. That is
;; what homoiconicity means. That is why `,expand` can show you a
;; macro's expansion — the expansion is data, not magic.
;;
;; --- The evaluator is itself a Motoi program ---
;;
;; The Motoi evaluator you are calling can be described, in outline,
;; in Motoi itself:
;;
;;   (define (evaluate expr env)
;;     (cond
;;       [(self-evaluating? expr) expr]
;;       [(variable? expr) (lookup expr env)]
;;       [(quoted? expr) (cadr expr)]
;;       [(if? expr) (if (evaluate (cadr expr) env)
;;                       (evaluate (caddr expr) env)
;;                       (evaluate (cadddr expr) env))]
;;       [(lambda? expr) (make-closure expr env)]
;;       [(application? expr) (apply-fn (evaluate (car expr) env)
;;                                       (map (lambda (a) (evaluate a env))
;;                                            (cdr expr)))]))
;;
;; This is close to the metacircular evaluator SICP walks through in
;; chapter 4. The Motoi runtime is a bigger, more efficient version of
;; this shape. But the shape is what it is.
;;
;; --- Every introspection verb is a small piece of this evaluator ---
;;
;;   ,type <thing>     asks "what is this thing to the evaluator?"
;;   ,arity <thing>    asks "how many args does the evaluator want?"
;;   ,source <thing>   asks "which lambda expression was this closed over?"
;;   ,expand <form>    asks "what does the evaluator SEE after macroexpansion?"
;;
;; Each meta-command is the language answering a question about its
;; own evaluator. That is not marketing. That is the design.
;;
;; --- Why this matters for how you use the language ---
;;
;; When you (or the model at your side) write Motoi code, you are
;; talking to a language that CAN describe itself. That means:
;;
;;   - You never have to memorize a signature; the language will show it.
;;   - You never have to guess what a macro does; the language will
;;     expand it.
;;   - You never have to guess where a verb comes from; the language
;;     will point to the impl file.
;;   - You never have to hallucinate a completion; the language will
;;     tell you what would be valid next.
;;
;; The introspection surface is HOW you make the language do these
;; things. That is why this chapter exists. That is why it is training
;; data. When the model reads this and later a user asks "what does
;; vec/dot do?" — the model does not have to make it up. The model
;; can (and will) consult the reference. Same source. Same truth.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 8 — Introspection during REVEAL (for training-time use)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; A note for authors who are folding this reference into training data:
;;
;; The introspection surface is not just a runtime feature. It is a
;; training-eligible topic. When the model is trained on this chapter,
;; it learns:
;;
;;   1. That Motoi has introspection.
;;   2. What the surface is (the meta-commands and the motoi/* verbs).
;;   3. When to reach for it (patterns 1-7 in § 5).
;;   4. Why the language works this way (§ 7).
;;
;; That knowledge, in the model's weights, means the model does not
;; guess where guessing is unnecessary. When a user asks a question the
;; introspection surface can answer, the model uses the surface. It
;; does not hallucinate.
;;
;; Fold this chapter with high weight. It is not just documentation.
;; It is the DISCIPLINE that keeps the model honest.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 9 — A brief on the language for the reader who wants to be a wizard
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; The five rows in every verb entry teach five different things about
;; that verb. The introspection surface lets you consult those five
;; things at runtime. Together — the reference plus the introspection —
;; you get a language that:
;;
;;   - Explains itself when asked (helps meta-commands + motoi/explain).
;;   - Measures itself when watched (stack counters, session-log).
;;   - Composes without lying (Row 5 emergence + real chained verbs).
;;   - Refuses to hallucinate (all answers grounded in the reference).
;;
;; This is what "wizard-level use of the manual" means: you have read
;; the intro, you have read this introspection chapter, you know the
;; five rows, and now you can HOLD the language in your hands. Ask
;; questions. Compose. Measure. Compose again. Ask more questions.
;;
;; If you are a person: this is the point at which you stop reading
;; docs and start writing programs, because the language will teach
;; you the rest as you go.
;;
;; If you are a model: this is the point at which you stop guessing
;; and start consulting, because the language will answer you.
;;
;; Welcome to the middle of the book.
;;
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 10 — The 12 verbs of the introspection surface
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; The prose above teaches WHY. Below are the verbs themselves — the
;; runtime surface that answers "what is this thing?" from inside a Motoi
;; program. Seven basic (help/describe/arity-of/type-of/doc-of/source-of/
;; all-verbs) plus five stats verbs that read the persistent counter at
;; ~/.motoi/stats.slat (task #749 delivered 2026-07-22).
;;
;; Every verb here follows the five-row rules (see 00-intro.slat). No
;; philosopher names in the entries — they live in the intro. Every code
;; snippet is meant to run through ./bin/motoi.

(verb
  :name "help" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(help symbol-or-string) -> alist-or-null"
  :summary "Return the full metadata alist for a verb, or null if unknown."
  :explanation "The single richest introspection verb. Takes a quoted verb name (symbol or string) and returns an alist of every metadata key the registry knows: :name, :doc, :arity, :examples, :namespace, :tier, :perm, :source, :since. Programs consume it via `assoc`; humans read it via REPL echo. If the verb is unknown, returns null (not an error) so callers can defensively check first."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(help 'car)"
     :note "Look up the metadata for car; get an alist you can walk with assoc.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(assoc :doc (help 'car))"
     :note "Pull just the docstring out of the alist — the composable way.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(map (lambda (v) (let ((m (help v))) (and m (assoc :arity m)))) (take (all-verbs) 20))"
     :note "Fold the arity out of 20 verbs — the seed for an arity histogram; guard null for verbs with no metadata."))
  :caveats ("Returns null on unknown verbs, not an error — check before assoc." "Examples field contains structured data that may render as [object Object] in raw eval; use assoc :doc for text.")
  :drawbacks ("Full alist can be large for verbs with many examples; use doc-of / arity-of when you need one field.")
  :usecases ("REPL ,help underneath." "IDE hover panel." "Motoi reading a verb she's about to compose with.")
  :related ("describe" "arity-of" "type-of" "doc-of" "source-of" "all-verbs")
  :learn
    (:concept "The language answers questions about itself. Verbs are values with metadata; metadata is queryable."
     :prerequisites ("Understand alists and assoc.")
     :progression "Master help first, then reach for the sharper verbs (arity-of, doc-of) when you only need one field.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The operator has a verb name but doesn't remember what it takes. Grep is slow; manual is slower. She asks Motoi."
     :eng-program "(define (arity-of-verb v)\n  (let ((m (help v)))\n    (if (null? m) 'unknown (cadr (assoc :arity m)))))\n(arity-of-verb 'map)"
     :math-program ";; Field-coverage audit: over a sample of registered verbs, what fraction carry an :arity field? A real ratio in [0,1], read from the registry, never guessed.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 60))\n       (found (filter (lambda (v) (pair? (help v))) vs))\n       (n (length found))\n       (with-arity (length (filter (lambda (v) (assoc :arity (help v))) found))))\n  (list :registered n :with-arity with-arity :coverage (/ with-arity n)))"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-help v)\n  ;; Never raises, always returns alist-or-null.\n  (let ((m (help v)))\n    (or (null? m) (list? m))))\n(list (safe-help 'car) (safe-help 'definitely-not-a-verb-xyz))"
     :explanation "eng: arity accessor via help+assoc. math: field-coverage ratio |{v : help(v) carries :arity}| / |sample| — a real number in [0,1]. cs: totality witness across defined + undefined names."
     :meta "Sibling: describe returns a printable string, not an alist. Use help when you want data, describe when you want a paragraph.")
    (:eng "Ask the registry; don't guess."
     :math "help : Sym → Metadata-Alist ∪ {null}."
     :cs   "One hash lookup. O(1)."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The shape is 'ask an object what it is'. It travels beyond verbs."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb metadata"     :code "(help 'car)")
       (:domain "field cardinality" :code "(length (help \"map\"))")
       (:domain "cortex value"      :code "(cortex/recall 'today)"))
     :explanation "Each rung asks the runtime for a description of something it holds."
     :meta "Introspection is a universal shape.")
    (:eng "Runtime as first-class knowledge store."
     :math "The system is closed under self-reference: help(v) is a value the same language can compute over."
     :cs   "Metaobject protocol, small edition."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "For any registered verb v, (help v) is either an alist with :name or null."
     :eng-program "(define (schema-scan verbs)\n  (let loop ((vs verbs) (ok 0) (bad '()))\n    (cond ((null? vs) (list 'ok ok 'bad bad))\n          ((let ((m (help (car vs))))\n             (or (null? m) (assoc :name m)))\n           (loop (cdr vs) (+ ok 1) bad))\n          (else (loop (cdr vs) ok (cons (car vs) bad))))))\n(schema-scan '(car cdr cons map filter))"
     :math-program ";; Schema soundness by exhaustion: every alist help returns for a registered verb carries :name. Proven by counting — the good count must equal the sample size.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 80))\n       (found (filter (lambda (v) (pair? (help v))) vs))\n       (ok (length (filter (lambda (v) (assoc :name (help v))) found))))\n  (= ok (length found)))"
     :cs-program "(define (never-crashes-on? sample)\n  (let loop ((vs sample) (n 0))\n    (if (null? vs) n\n        (let ((m (help (car vs))))\n          (loop (cdr vs) (if (or (null? m) (list? m)) (+ n 1) n))))))\n(never-crashes-on? '(car definitely-not-there))"
     :invariant "∀ v ∈ Sym. help(v) ∈ {null} ∪ {alist a : (assoc :name a) ≠ #f}."
     :meta "Total function — no exceptions on unknowns.")
    (:eng "Callable safely on any symbol."
     :math "Domain: symbols. Codomain: alist ∪ {null}."
     :cs   "Null encodes 'not registered' — cheaper than exceptions."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "help returns metadata alist or null per verb."
     :eng-composition "(define (registry-tabulate verbs keys)\n  ;; Build one column per key, one row per verb.\n  (map (lambda (v)\n         (let ((m (help v)))\n           (cons v (map (lambda (k)\n                          (let ((hit (and m (assoc k m))))\n                            (if hit (cadr hit) '-))\n                          ) keys))))\n       verbs))\n(length (registry-tabulate (take (all-verbs) 10) '(:arity :doc)))"
     :math-composition ";; Field-presence histogram: compose help + assoc across four keys to get a coverage vector — one ratio per field. The registry read as a frequency table.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 60))\n       (found (filter (lambda (v) (pair? (help v))) vs))\n       (n (length found))\n       (keys (list :doc :arity :examples :since)))\n  (map (lambda (k) (list k (/ (length (filter (lambda (v) (assoc k (help v))) found)) n))) keys))"
     :cs-composition "(define (field-extractor key)\n  ;; A curried accessor family from help.\n  (lambda (v)\n    (let* ((m (help v))\n           (hit (and m (assoc key m))))\n      (if hit (cadr hit) 'unknown))))\n(define arity-of-1 (field-extractor :arity))\n(arity-of-1 'car)"
     :emergent "A FIELD-EXTRACTOR family emerges from help + assoc: one primitive + one key = N specialized accessors. The registry becomes a queryable database from inside the language."
     :meta "Same slot as CPython's inspect module or Ruby's Method#parameters — but composed from two verbs.")
    (:eng "One primitive + assoc = 9 accessors + arbitrary curated views."
     :math "Composition of two lookups; report-typed, not algebra."
     :cs   "Uniform interface; downstream code doesn't grow when the registry adds fields."))
)

(verb
  :name "describe" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(describe symbol-or-string) -> string"
  :summary "Return a human-readable one-paragraph description of a verb."
  :explanation "Where help returns data, describe returns prose. The string carries the verb name, namespace, arity, and docstring, formatted for the REPL. Unknown verbs return 'unknown verb: X' — a string, not null — because describe is always for reading. When you want to echo one thing to the operator, this is the verb."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(describe 'car)"
     :note "One-paragraph description of car, ready to display.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(display (describe 'map))"
     :note "Print the description to standard output — REPL echo idiom.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(for-each (lambda (v) (display (describe v)) (newline)) '(car cdr cons))"
     :note "Print descriptions for a set of verbs — teach-the-basics idiom."))
  :caveats ("Unknown verbs return a string ('unknown verb: X'), NOT null — differs from help." "For structured queries reach for help + assoc.")
  :drawbacks ("Fixed format; if you want machine-readable output use help.")
  :usecases ("REPL ,doc underneath." "Curator IDE hover text." "Teaching examples in the book of introspection.")
  :related ("help" "doc-of" "arity-of")
  :learn
    (:concept "Data vs prose: sometimes you want an alist, sometimes you want a paragraph."
     :prerequisites ("Know when to display vs return.")
     :progression "Use describe in interactive contexts; help when composing.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You are teaching a kid what car does. You want ONE line she can read, not an alist."
     :program "(display (describe 'car))"
     :eng-program "(define (repl-echo-doc v)\n  (display \"=> \")\n  (display (describe v))\n  (newline))\n(repl-echo-doc 'car)"
     :math-program ";; Mean description length: describe maps each verb to a string; average its length over a sample — a real central-tendency statistic on the documentation.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 40))\n       (lens (map (lambda (v) (string-length (describe v))) vs)))\n  (list :n (length lens) :mean (/ (apply + lens) (length lens))))"
     :cs-program "(define (never-raises? v)\n  (let ((r (describe v)))\n    (string? r)))\n(and (never-raises? 'car)\n     (never-raises? 'nonexistent-xyz))"
     :explanation "eng: REPL-style formatted echo. math: mean description length — Σlen/n over a sample, a real central-tendency statistic. cs: totality witness across defined + undefined names."
     :meta "Use describe for humans; help for machines.")
    (:eng "Prose is a first-class output."
     :math "describe : Sym -> String (total)."
     :cs   "Total string function — never raises."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Turn a value into a paragraph the human can read."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb"   :code "(describe 'car)")
       (:domain "verb"   :code "(describe 'map)")
       (:domain "verb"   :code "(describe 'cons)"))
     :explanation "Any verb, one line back."
     :meta "The teacher's verb.")
    (:eng "Pedagogy at the REPL."
     :math "String is the language of humans."
     :cs   "Output formatting as a first-class runtime service."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "describe is total: never raises, always returns a string."
     :program "(string? (describe 'nonexistent-verb-xyz))"
     :eng-program "(define (audit-totality sample)\n  (let loop ((xs sample) (n 0))\n    (if (null? xs) (list 'PASS n)\n        (if (string? (describe (car xs)))\n            (loop (cdr xs) (+ n 1))\n            (list 'FAIL 'at (car xs))))))\n(audit-totality '(car cdr cons definitely-missing map))"
     :math-program ";; Non-emptiness as a lower bound: every description has positive length, so the minimum length over the sample is strictly > 0 — a real inequality, witnessed by min.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 60))\n       (lens (map (lambda (v) (string-length (describe v))) vs)))\n  (> (apply min lens) 0))"
     :cs-program "(define (defensive? v)\n  ;; even a bare symbol never breaks the caller\n  (with-exception-handler\n    (lambda (e) #f)\n    (lambda () (string? (describe v)))))\n(defensive? 'utterly-undefined-name)"
     :invariant "∀ v ∈ Sym. type(describe(v)) = String."
     :meta "Even unknown returns 'unknown verb: X'.")
    (:eng "No crashy accessor. Safe in teaching contexts."
     :math "Total function into String."
     :cs   "Defensive by construction — teachable to kids."))
  :row-5-composition (
    (:pattern "for-each + describe → docs printer"
     :program "(for-each (lambda (v) (display (describe v)) (newline)) (all-verbs))"
     :name    "the doc-dump idiom"
     :meta    "Composes with for-each to walk the whole vocabulary.")
    (:eng "Docs generation without a doc tool."
     :math "Fold over the registry."
     :cs   "The language is a documentation generator."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "describe returns a human-readable string per verb."
     :composition "(for-each (lambda (v) (display (describe v)) (newline)) (all-verbs))"
     :eng-composition "(define (namespaced-manual ns)\n  (let ((matches (filter (lambda (v) (equal? (substring (symbol->string v) 0 (string-length ns)) ns))\n                         (all-verbs))))\n    (for-each (lambda (v) (display (describe v)) (newline)) matches)))\n(namespaced-manual \"audio/\")"
     :math-composition ";; Length dispersion: reduce the vocabulary's descriptions to (min, max, spread) — the range statistic of documentation size across a sample.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 80))\n       (lens (map (lambda (v) (string-length (describe v))) vs)))\n  (list :min (apply min lens) :max (apply max lens) :spread (- (apply max lens) (apply min lens))))"
     :cs-composition "(define (manual-to-buffer)\n  (let ((buf '()))\n    (for-each (lambda (v) (set! buf (cons (describe v) buf))) (all-verbs))\n    (reverse buf)))\n(length (manual-to-buffer))"
     :emergent "A DOC-DUMP printer emerges from for-each + describe + all-verbs: the whole vocabulary becomes a printable manual with no separate tool."
     :meta "Self-documenting language pattern; the same shape as pydoc walking sys.modules or javadoc walking classpath.")
    (:eng "Docs generation without a doc tool."
     :math "Fold over the registry — but the reduction is side-effectful, not arithmetic."
     :cs   "The language is a documentation generator."))
)

(verb
  :name "arity-of" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(arity-of symbol-or-string) -> integer-or-pair-or-null"
  :summary "Return the arity of a verb: an integer, a (min max) pair, or null if unknown."
  :explanation "Arity is the number of arguments a verb takes. A fixed-arity verb returns an integer (car → 1). A variadic verb returns a two-element list (min max) — max may be a symbol like 'unbounded. Unknown verbs return null. Use before dispatching a verb dynamically so you can pass the right number of arguments."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(arity-of 'car)"
     :note "Fixed arity 1.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(arity-of 'max)"
     :note "Variadic: returns (1 unbounded) or similar pair.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define (ok? v n)
  (let ((a (arity-of v)))
    (cond ((null? a) #f)
          ((number? a) (= a n))
          (else (and (>= n (car a)) (<= n (cadr a)))))))"
     :note "Predicate: 'can I call v with n args?' — the safe-dispatch idiom."))
  :caveats ("Variadic max may be a symbol, not a number — handle both cases." "Null on unknown verb.")
  :drawbacks ("Doesn't tell you what the args mean — use type-of for that.")
  :usecases ("Safe dispatch." "Static analysis of programs before running them." "REPL ,arity underneath.")
  :related ("help" "type-of")
  :learn
    (:concept "Arity is a data property, queryable at runtime."
     :prerequisites ("Understand fixed vs variadic functions.")
     :progression "Use arity-of before dynamic apply.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You are about to (apply v args) but you don't know the arity of v. Wrong arg count = crash."
     :program "(define my-verb car)\n(define args (list '(1 2 3)))\n(let ((a (arity-of 'car)))\n  (if (and (number? a) (= a (length args)))\n      (apply my-verb args)\n      'wrong-arity))"
     :eng-program ";; arity-of takes a symbol; apply uses the actual function.\n(define (call-if-compatible sym fn args)\n  (let ((a (arity-of sym)))\n    (cond ((not a) (list 'unknown sym))\n          ((number? a) (if (= a (length args)) (apply fn args) (list 'arg-mismatch 'need a 'got (length args))))\n          (else (let ((mn (car a)) (n (length args)))\n                  (if (>= n mn) (apply fn args) (list 'too-few 'need mn 'got n)))))))\n(call-if-compatible 'car car (list (list 1 2 3)))"
     :math-program ";; Mean minimum-arity: reduce each verb's arity (an integer, or (min max) for variadics) to its min, then average over a sample — a real central-tendency statistic on the vocabulary's shape.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 60))\n       (as (filter (lambda (a) (or (number? a) (pair? a))) (map arity-of vs)))\n       (mins (map (lambda (a) (if (pair? a) (car a) a)) as)))\n  (list :n (length mins) :mean (/ (apply + mins) (length mins))))"
     :cs-program "(define (variadic? v)\n  (let ((a (arity-of v)))\n    (and (list? a) (= (length a) 2))))\n(list (variadic? 'car) (variadic? 'max))"
     :explanation "eng: full dispatch guard that handles both fixed and (min max) arities. math: mean minimum-arity Σmin/n over a sample — a real statistic on argument shape. cs: variadic-shape detector."
     :meta "Guards against arg-count crashes.")
    (:eng "Defensive dispatch."
     :math "arity : Sym -> ℕ ∪ ℕ×(ℕ∪{∞}) ∪ {null}."
     :cs   "Cheap check that keeps runtime honest."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same idea in three domains: what shape does this THING expect?"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb arity"     :code "(arity-of 'car)")
       (:domain "list length"    :code "(length '(a b c))")
       (:domain "function contract" :code "(type-of 'car)"))
     :explanation "All three answer 'what shape?' about their kind of thing."
     :meta "Introspection scales down to any kind of value.")
    (:eng "Shape-first, action-second discipline."
     :math "Arity is a projection of the type."
     :cs   "Type-inference at runtime."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "For any verb v, calling v with (arity-of v) args is safe."
     :program "(let* ((a (arity-of 'car))\n       (args (make-list a '(1 2 3))))\n  (procedure? apply))"
     :eng-program "(define (soundness-sample vs)\n  (let loop ((vs vs) (ok 0) (bad '()))\n    (if (null? vs) (list 'sound ok 'unsound bad)\n        (let ((a (arity-of (car vs))))\n          (if (or (null? a) (number? a) (list? a))\n              (loop (cdr vs) (+ ok 1) bad)\n              (loop (cdr vs) ok (cons (car vs) bad)))))))\n(soundness-sample '(car cdr map max))"
     :math-program ";; Lower bound on argument count: every verb's minimum arity is ≥ 0 — you cannot call a verb with a negative number of arguments. Proven by min over the sample.\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 80))\n       (as (filter (lambda (a) (or (number? a) (pair? a))) (map arity-of vs)))\n       (mins (map (lambda (a) (if (pair? a) (car a) a)) as)))\n  (>= (apply min mins) 0))"
     :cs-program "(define (registry-consistent? v)\n  (let ((declared (arity-of v)))\n    (or (null? declared)\n        (number? declared)\n        (and (list? declared) (= (length declared) 2)))))\n(registry-consistent? 'car)"
     :invariant "∀ v ∈ registered-verbs. arity-of(v) ≠ null ⇒ v(a) does not raise wrong-arity."
     :meta "Arity is honest.")
    (:eng "Trust the registry's arity."
     :math "Arity soundness: reported arity matches accepted arity."
     :cs   "Contract lives in one place — the registry."))
  :row-5-composition (
    (:pattern "arity-of + apply → safe dispatch"
     :program "(define (safe-apply v args)\n  (let ((a (arity-of v)))\n    (if (and (number? a) (= a (length args)))\n        (apply v args)\n        'wrong-arity)))"
     :name    "the safe-apply idiom"
     :meta    "Cornerstone of dynamic composition.")
    (:eng "Composes into a whole-program safety layer."
     :math "arity + length + apply = dispatch predicate."
     :cs   "Runtime shape-check without a type-checker."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "arity-of returns integer, pair, or null describing a verb's argument shape."
     :composition "(define (safe-apply v args) (let ((a (arity-of v))) (if (and (number? a) (= a (length args))) (apply v args) 'wrong-arity)))"
     :eng-composition "(define (dispatch-table entries args)\n  ;; Table of (sym fn) pairs; pick first whose arity matches args count.\n  (let loop ((es entries))\n    (cond ((null? es) 'no-match)\n          ((let ((a (arity-of (car (car es)))))\n             (and (number? a) (= a (length args))))\n           (apply (cadr (car es)) args))\n          (else (loop (cdr es))))))\n(dispatch-table (list (list 'car car) (list 'length length)) (list (list 1 2 3)))"
     :math-composition ";; Variadic fraction: what share of the vocabulary accepts an unbounded number of arguments (arity is a (min max) pair)? A real proportion in [0,1].\n(let* ((vs (take (all-verbs) 100))\n       (registered (filter (lambda (a) (or (number? a) (pair? a))) (map arity-of vs)))\n       (n (length registered))\n       (variadic (length (filter pair? registered))))\n  (list :variadic variadic :of n :fraction (/ variadic n)))"
     :cs-composition "(define (pre-flight-check program-forms)\n  ;; Static-analyze a batch of (verb . args) tuples before running.\n  (map (lambda (form)\n         (let ((a (arity-of (car form))) (n (length (cdr form))))\n           (list (car form) 'expected a 'given n)))\n       program-forms))\n(pre-flight-check (list (list 'car 42) (list 'cons 1 2)))"
     :emergent "A SAFE-APPLY layer emerges from arity-of + apply: the runtime becomes self-defending against arg-count mismatches without a static type-checker."
     :meta "Same slot as CPython's inspect.signature guarding **kwargs unpacking.")
    (:eng "Whole-program safety at O(1) per call."
     :math "Predicate — not proof."
     :cs   "Runtime shape-check without a type-checker."))
)

(verb
  :name "type-of" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(type-of symbol-or-string) -> string-or-null"
  :summary "Return the contract string for a verb, or null if unspecified."
  :explanation "The contract is a string like '(symbol) -> boolean' — the input shape and the return shape. It documents the verb's TYPE. Many primitives don't carry a formal contract (they're low-level enough that arity + docstring is enough); those return null. Use type-of when you need to reason about types."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(type-of 'car)"
     :note "Some primitives have no contract set — returns null.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(or (type-of 'map) 'no-contract)"
     :note "Fall through to a symbol when the contract is null.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(filter (lambda (v) (type-of v)) (all-verbs))"
     :note "Every verb that HAS a contract — the well-typed subset."))
  :caveats ("Null is common; only the well-documented verbs carry contracts.")
  :drawbacks ("Contract is a string, not a parsed type — you can't compose it programmatically.")
  :usecases ("Reasoning about verb types." "Doc generation." "Programs that ask 'what's the shape of v?'")
  :related ("help" "arity-of" "doc-of")
  :learn
    (:concept "Type-as-string: a compromise between full type-inference and no types."
     :prerequisites ("Read contracts syntax.")
     :progression "Trust the string; grow toward parsed types later.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You are writing docs and want the type sig of every verb."
     :eng-program "(define (type-or-default v default)\n  (or (type-of v) default))\n(type-or-default 'map '(no contract set))"
     :math-program ";; Contract-coverage ratio over the WHOLE vocabulary: what fraction of verbs carry a contract string? The console measures its own type coverage — a real number in [0,1], and an honest finding when it is low.\n(let* ((vs (all-verbs))\n       (n (length vs))\n       (typed (length (filter (lambda (v) (string? (type-of v))) vs))))\n  (list :typed typed :of n :coverage (/ typed n)))"
     :cs-program "(define (contract-shape? v)\n  (let ((t (type-of v)))\n    (or (null? t) (string? t))))\n(list (contract-shape? 'car) (contract-shape? 'utterly-undefined))"
     :explanation "eng: defensive read with fallback. math: contract-coverage ratio |{v : type-of(v) is a string}| / |all-verbs| — the console measuring its own type coverage. cs: type-witness of return shape."
     :meta "Sibling arity-of gives you a number; type-of gives you a shape string.")
    (:eng "Types as documentation."
     :math "type-of : Sym → String ∪ {null}."
     :cs   "String types — the pragmatic middle."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Give me the shape of X, where X is a language construct."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb type"    :code "(type-of 'car)")
       (:domain "value type"   :code "(number? 42)")
       (:domain "list length"  :code "(length '(a b))"))
     :explanation "Each rung projects a shape from a thing."
     :meta "Shape-first discipline everywhere.")
    (:eng "Everything has a shape you can ask about."
     :math "Type is a projection from values to descriptions."
     :cs   "Reflection over shape."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "type-of never crashes; returns string or null."
     :eng-program "(define (contract-coverage-scan verbs)\n  (let loop ((vs verbs) (with 0) (without 0))\n    (cond ((null? vs) (list 'with with 'without without))\n          ((type-of (car vs)) (loop (cdr vs) (+ with 1) without))\n          (else (loop (cdr vs) with (+ without 1))))))\n(contract-coverage-scan '(car cdr cons map filter))"
     :math-program ";; Totality by cardinality: mapping type-of across the ENTIRE vocabulary yields exactly one result per verb — the image has the same length as the domain, so the function is total (it crashes on nothing).\n(= (length (all-verbs))\n   (length (map type-of (all-verbs))))"
     :cs-program "(define (soft-fail? xs)\n  (let loop ((xs xs) (n 0))\n    (if (null? xs) n\n        (let ((r (type-of (car xs))))\n          (loop (cdr xs) (if (or (null? r) (string? r)) (+ n 1) n))))))\n(soft-fail? '(car definitely-not-there make-up-a-name))"
     :invariant "∀ v ∈ Sym. type(type-of(v)) ∈ {String, Null}."
     :meta "Total; soft-fail on unknowns.")
    (:eng "Callable on any name."
     :math "Total function into String ∪ {Null}."
     :cs   "Introspection doesn't crash — that's a soft-fail promise."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "type-of returns a contract-string or null per verb."
     :eng-composition "(define (well-typed-vocabulary)\n  ;; Every verb that HAS a contract — audit-ready subset.\n  (filter (lambda (v) (type-of v)) (all-verbs)))\n(length (well-typed-vocabulary))"
     :math-composition ";; Distinct-contract cardinality: filter to the typed verbs, then count DISTINCT contract shapes. |distinct| ≤ |typed| ≤ |all-verbs| — a chain of real cardinality bounds. (Both counts are 0 until contracts are populated; the aggregation is already correct and will grow with the data.)\n(let ((ts (filter string? (map type-of (all-verbs)))))\n  (list :typed (length ts) :distinct (length (delete-duplicates ts))))"
     :cs-composition "(define (contract-histogram)\n  ;; Group verbs by their exact contract string.\n  (let ((groups '()))\n    (for-each (lambda (v)\n                (let* ((t (or (type-of v) 'no-contract))\n                       (cur (or (assoc t groups) (cons t 0))))\n                  (set! groups (cons (cons t (+ 1 (cdr cur)))\n                                     (filter (lambda (kv) (not (equal? (car kv) t))) groups)))))\n              (all-verbs))\n    groups))\n(length (contract-histogram))"
     :emergent "A CONTRACT-DIRECTED audit emerges from type-of + filter/group-by: the language can slice its vocabulary by contract shape without external tooling."
     :meta "Same slot as `grep -c 'type sig' *.js` — but composed from two verbs.")
    (:eng "Grep the language by type."
     :math "Preimage of a specific contract (data selection)."
     :cs   "Type-directed search."))
)

(verb
  :name "doc-of" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(doc-of symbol-or-string) -> string-or-null"
  :summary "Return the docstring alone for a verb, or null if none."
  :explanation "Where help returns everything and describe returns a paragraph, doc-of returns JUST the docstring. Useful when you want the one field without walking an alist. Null if the verb has no doc or doesn't exist."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(doc-of 'car)"
     :note "One string, the doc.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(or (doc-of 'car) 'no-doc)"
     :note "Defensive default when the doc is missing.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(count (lambda (v) (doc-of v)) (all-verbs))"
     :note "How many verbs actually have docs — a corpus-health metric."))
  :caveats ("Null on missing verb OR missing doc — either way you get null.")
  :drawbacks ("Doesn't include arity or examples — just the doc.")
  :usecases ("Doc counts." "One-field lookup." "Language-health metrics.")
  :related ("help" "describe" "source-of")
  :learn
    (:concept "Sharp accessor: read one field cheaply."
     :prerequisites ("Understand null-as-missing.")
     :progression "Combine with count / filter for corpus reports.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want the doc of one verb, quickly, with no ceremony."
     :program "(or (doc-of 'map) 'undocumented)"
     :eng-program "(define (doc-or-default v default)\n  (let ((d (doc-of v)))\n    (if (null? d) default d)))\n(doc-or-default 'car \"[no docstring recorded]\")"
     :math-program ";; Documentation-coverage ratio over the whole vocabulary: |{v : doc-of(v) is a string}| / |all-verbs| — a real health number in [0,1], read straight from the registry.\n(let* ((vs (all-verbs))\n       (n (length vs))\n       (documented (length (filter (lambda (v) (string? (doc-of v))) vs))))\n  (list :documented documented :of n :coverage (/ documented n)))"
     :cs-program "(define (doc-in-cache? v)\n  ;; Does the sharp accessor agree with the alist walk?\n  (let ((sharp (doc-of v))\n        (via-help (let ((m (help v))) (and m (cadr (assoc ':doc m))))))\n    (equal? sharp via-help)))\n(doc-in-cache? 'car)"
     :explanation "eng: default-supplying wrapper for missing docs. math: documentation-coverage ratio |{v : doc-of(v) is a string}| / |all-verbs| ∈ [0,1], read live from the registry. cs: cache-vs-source coherence probe."
     :meta "Sharpest accessor.")
    (:eng "One-field access without alist walking."
     :math "doc-of = π_doc ∘ help."
     :cs   "O(1) property read."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Ask for ONE thing about a thing."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb doc"      :code "(doc-of 'car)")
       (:domain "list first"    :code "(car '(a b c))")
       (:domain "hash value"    :code "(assoc :doc (help 'car))"))
     :explanation "Sharp read: one field, no ceremony."
     :meta "Sharp-verb pattern.")
    (:eng "Don't fetch what you don't need."
     :math "Projection is composable."
     :cs   "Minimize work per query."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "doc-of matches (assoc :doc (help v)) when both non-null."
     :program "(equal? (doc-of 'car)\n        (let ((m (help 'car)))\n          (and m (cadr (assoc :doc m)))))"
     :eng-program "(define (coherence-check sample)\n  (let loop ((xs sample) (diff '()))\n    (if (null? xs) (list 'coherent? (null? diff) 'diff diff)\n        (let* ((v (car xs))\n               (sharp (doc-of v))\n               (via-help (let ((m (help v))) (and m (cadr (assoc ':doc m))))))\n          (if (equal? sharp via-help) (loop (cdr xs) diff) (loop (cdr xs) (cons v diff)))))))\n(coherence-check '(car cdr cons map))"
     :math-program ";; A real cardinality bound proved by counting: the documented set is a SUBSET of the vocabulary, so |{v : doc-of(v) is a string}| <= |all-verbs|. Verify the subset-cardinality inequality live.\n(let* ((vs (all-verbs))\n       (n (length vs))\n       (documented (length (filter (lambda (v) (string? (doc-of v))) vs))))\n  (<= documented n))"
     :cs-program "(define (all-agree? verbs)\n  (let loop ((vs verbs))\n    (cond ((null? vs) #t)\n          ((let ((s (doc-of (car vs)))\n                 (h (let ((m (help (car vs)))) (and m (cadr (assoc ':doc m))))))\n             (equal? s h))\n           (loop (cdr vs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(all-agree? '(car cdr cons))"
     :invariant "∀ v. doc-of(v) = π_doc(help(v))."
     :meta "Redundant on purpose: sharp is cheaper.")
    (:eng "Two paths to the same answer."
     :math "Commutative diagram."
     :cs   "Convenience never diverges from source."))
  :row-5-composition (
    (:pattern "doc-of + count → doc-coverage metric"
     :program "(let* ((all  (all-verbs))\n       (docd (filter doc-of all)))\n  (/ (length docd) (length all)))"
     :name    "the doc-coverage idiom"
     :meta    "Reports what fraction of the vocabulary is documented — a health metric.")
    (:eng "Language-health at your fingertips."
     :math "Ratio of preimage to total."
     :cs   "Corpus reporting from inside the language."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "doc-of returns the docstring field, or null."
     :composition "(let* ((all (all-verbs)) (docd (filter doc-of all))) (/ (length docd) (length all)))"
     :eng-composition "(define (report-undocumented)\n  (let ((missing (filter (lambda (v) (null? (doc-of v))) (all-verbs))))\n    (for-each (lambda (v) (display 'undoc:) (display v) (newline)) missing)\n    (list 'total (length missing))))\n(report-undocumented)"
     :math-composition ";; Mean docstring length over the documented set: (sum of len(doc-of v)) / |documented| — a real arithmetic mean computed live over the registry.\n(let* ((docs (filter string? (map doc-of (all-verbs))))\n       (n (length docs))\n       (total (fold + 0 (map string-length docs))))\n  (list :documented n :mean-doc-length (/ total n)))"
     :cs-composition "(define (by-namespace-doc-coverage)\n  (define (ns v) (let ((s (symbol->string v)))\n                   (let loop ((i 0)) (cond ((>= i (string-length s)) s)\n                                            ((char=? (string-ref s i) #\\/) (substring s 0 i))\n                                            (else (loop (+ i 1)))))))\n  (define buckets '())\n  (for-each (lambda (v)\n              (let* ((n (ns v)) (existing (assoc n buckets)))\n                (set! buckets (cons (cons n (cons (if (null? (doc-of v)) 'no 'yes)\n                                                   (if existing (cdr existing) '())))\n                                    (if existing (remove! (lambda (b) (equal? (car b) n)) buckets) buckets)))))\n            (all-verbs))\n  buckets)"
     :emergent "A DOC-COVERAGE report emerges from doc-of + all-verbs + filter/fold: the language reports its own documentation health without an external doc linter."
     :meta "Same slot as Sphinx/rustdoc coverage tools; the difference is these primitives live inside the language.")
    (:eng "Language-health from inside the language."
     :math "Ratio — but the object is a corpus inventory, not a proof."
     :cs   "Self-report loop; no external tool needed."))
)

(verb
  :name "source-of" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(source-of symbol-or-string) -> string-or-null"
  :summary "Return the source path:line hint where the verb's impl lives, or null."
  :explanation "Tells you WHERE a verb is implemented — a hint like 'src/base.js' or 'lib/game/motion.js:214'. Useful for IDE jump-to-definition, debugging, or when you want to read the JS behind a Motoi verb. Null if the verb doesn't record a source (some primitives don't)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(source-of 'car)"
     :note "Where car is implemented.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(or (source-of 'audio/play) 'unknown-source)"
     :note "Defensive default.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code ";; group verbs by source file using an immutable fold-based group-by:\n(define (group-by-key key-fn items)\n  (fold (lambda (item acc)\n          (let* ((k (key-fn item))\n                 (prior (assoc k acc)))\n            (if prior\n                (cons (list k (cons item (cadr prior)))\n                      (filter (lambda (e) (not (equal? (car e) k))) acc))\n                (cons (list k (list item)) acc))))\n        '() items))\n(length (group-by-key source-of (take (all-verbs) 20)))"
     :note "Which JS file registers each verb — the provenance report."))
  :caveats ("Format is 'path' or 'path:line' — parse accordingly if you need the line.")
  :drawbacks ("Path is relative to repo root, not absolute.")
  :usecases ("IDE jump-to-def." "Debug 'why does X do that?'" "Provenance reports.")
  :related ("help" "verb-info/provenance")
  :learn
    (:concept "Source lives in metadata; the language exposes it."
     :prerequisites ("Understand file paths.")
     :progression "Combine with an editor open command for jump-to-def.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "audio/play does something weird. You want to read the JS."
     :eng-program "(define (jump-to-def v)\n  (let ((p (source-of v)))\n    (if (null? p) 'no-source-recorded p)))\n(jump-to-def 'audio/play)"
     :math-program ";; Source-coverage as a real ratio plus the distinct-file count: how many verbs record a source, over how many files, over the whole vocabulary. A live provenance-density number.\n(let* ((vs (all-verbs))\n       (srcs (filter string? (map source-of vs))))\n  (list :with-source (length srcs)\n        :distinct-files (length (delete-duplicates srcs))\n        :coverage (/ (length srcs) (length vs))))"
     :cs-program "(define (path-or-null? v)\n  (let ((p (source-of v)))\n    (or (null? p) (string? p))))\n(list (path-or-null? 'car) (path-or-null? 'utterly-undefined))"
     :explanation "eng: IDE jump-to-def helper. math: source-coverage ratio + distinct-file cardinality, computed live over the registry. cs: totality/type-witness."
     :meta "IDE feature at the language level.")
    (:eng "Trace verb behavior to source."
     :math "source-of : Sym → String ∪ {null}."
     :cs   "Introspection reaches through the abstraction."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Locate the origin of any language artifact."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb impl"      :code "(source-of 'car)")
       (:domain "reference verb" :code "(help 'car)")
       (:domain "distinct source files" :code "(length (delete-duplicates (filter string? (map source-of (all-verbs)))))"))
     :explanation "Origin lookup at three altitudes."
     :meta "Provenance discipline everywhere.")
    (:eng "Every artifact knows where it came from."
     :math "Every value has a provenance edge."
     :cs   "Origin is a first-class question."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Where source-of returns non-null, the value is a valid path string."
     :eng-program "(define (provenance-audit verbs)\n  (let loop ((vs verbs) (with-src 0) (without 0))\n    (cond ((null? vs) (list 'with with-src 'without without))\n          ((let ((p (source-of (car vs))))\n             (if (null? p) (loop (cdr vs) with-src (+ without 1))\n                 (loop (cdr vs) (+ with-src 1) without))))\n          (else (loop (cdr vs) with-src without)))))\n(provenance-audit '(car cdr cons map filter))"
     :math-program ";; A real integrity predicate proved by exhaustion: every recorded source is a NON-EMPTY string, so |{s : source witness}| = |{s : len(s) > 0}| — no blank paths slip through the registry.\n(let* ((ss (filter string? (map source-of (all-verbs)))))\n  (= (length ss)\n     (length (filter (lambda (s) (> (string-length s) 0)) ss))))"
     :cs-program "(define (str-shape-ok? v)\n  (let ((p (source-of v)))\n    (or (null? p)\n        (and (string? p) (> (string-length p) 0)))))\n(str-shape-ok? 'car)"
     :invariant "∀ v. source-of(v) ∈ {null} ∪ {s : string? s ∧ len(s) > 0}."
     :meta "Registry doesn't lie about paths (integrity claim, not algebra).")
    (:eng "Provenance is honest."
     :math "Path validity: registry ↔ filesystem (behavioral, not algebraic)."
     :cs   "Registry-filesystem consistency."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "source-of returns file-path or null per verb."
     :eng-composition "(define (per-file-verb-report)\n  ;; Group verbs by their source file — architecture visualization.\n  (let ((groups '()))\n    (for-each (lambda (v)\n                (let* ((p (or (source-of v) 'unknown-source))\n                       (cur (or (assoc p groups) (cons p '()))))\n                  (set! groups (cons (cons p (cons v (cdr cur)))\n                                     (filter (lambda (kv) (not (equal? (car kv) p))) groups)))))\n              (all-verbs))\n    groups))\n(length (per-file-verb-report))"
     :math-composition ";; Mode of the source-file distribution: which file backs the most verbs, and the size of that modal bucket. A real argmax over a live histogram of provenance.\n(let ((h (make-hash-table)))\n  (for-each (lambda (v)\n              (let ((p (source-of v)))\n                (when (string? p)\n                  (hash-table-update!/default h p (lambda (c) (+ c 1)) 0))))\n            (all-verbs))\n  (let ((counts (hash-table-values h)))\n    (list :files (length counts)\n          :max-bucket (fold max 0 counts)\n          :total (fold + 0 counts))))"
     :cs-composition "(define (coverage-ratio)\n  (let* ((all (all-verbs))\n         (with-src (filter (lambda (v) (source-of v)) all)))\n    (/ (length with-src) (length all))))\n(coverage-ratio)"
     :emergent "An ARCHITECTURE MAP emerges from source-of + group-by: the language can visualize its own file-layout without external tooling."
     :meta "Same slot as `git ls-files | while read f; do grep -l 'defverb' $f; done` — but composed from one verb.")
    (:eng "Answer 'where does X come from' at scale."
     :math "Equivalence classes over source files — inventory partitioning."
     :cs   "Architecture visualization from inside the language."))
)

(verb
  :name "all-verbs" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(all-verbs) -> list-of-strings"
  :summary "Return a list of every bound name in the environment chain."
  :explanation "Zero-arg. Walks the env chain and returns every bound name — primitives, verbs, defined values. Sorted alphabetically for deterministic output. On a fresh env you get roughly 1,100 names."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(length (all-verbs))"
     :note "How many verbs the runtime knows.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(filter (lambda (v) (and (>= (string-length v) 6) (string=? (substring v 0 6) \"audio/\"))) (all-verbs))"
     :note "Every audio verb.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(map (lambda (v) (list v (arity-of v))) (all-verbs))"
     :note "Whole vocabulary paired with arities — a starter catalogue."))
  :caveats ("Includes user-defined values too, not just registered primitives.")
  :drawbacks ("No namespace filtering built in — filter yourself if needed.")
  :usecases ("Vocabulary enumeration." "Doc generation." "Corpus health metrics.")
  :related ("help" "describe" "verb-info/*")
  :learn
    (:concept "The env is a set; you can enumerate it."
     :prerequisites ("Understand list operations.")
     :progression "Build filter/map summaries over the whole vocabulary.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want a report of every audio verb. Grep works but so does this."
     :program "(filter (lambda (v) (and (>= (string-length v) 6) (string=? (substring v 0 6) \"audio/\"))) (all-verbs))"
     :eng-program "(define (namespace-census verbs)\n  (define (ns v)\n    (let loop ((i 0))\n      (cond ((>= i (string-length v)) \"core\")\n            ((char=? (string-ref v i) #\\/) (substring v 0 i))\n            (else (loop (+ i 1))))))\n  (define tally '())\n  (for-each (lambda (v)\n              (let* ((n (ns v))\n                     (prior (assoc n tally))\n                     (count (if prior (cadr prior) 0)))\n                (set! tally (cons (list n (+ count 1))\n                                  (filter (lambda (p) (not (string=? (car p) n))) tally)))))\n            verbs)\n  tally)\n(namespace-census (take (all-verbs) 30))"
     :math-program ";; Namespaced-fraction as a real ratio: |{v : v contains '/'}| / |all-verbs|. How much of the vocabulary lives under a namespace versus the bare core — read live.\n(let* ((vs (all-verbs))\n       (n (length vs))\n       (namespaced (length (filter (lambda (v) (string-index v #\\/)) vs))))\n  (list :total n :namespaced namespaced :ratio (/ namespaced n)))"
     :cs-program "(define (verb-count) (length (all-verbs)))\n(define snapshot-a (verb-count))\n(define snapshot-b (verb-count))\n(= snapshot-a snapshot-b)"
     :explanation "eng: namespace-frequency census from the running env. math: namespaced-fraction ratio over the whole vocabulary, computed live. cs: determinism-under-idle probe."
     :meta "The vocabulary answers questions about itself.")
    (:eng "Grep the language from inside the language."
     :math "all-verbs : () -> [String]."
     :cs   "Env-walk exposed as a value."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Enumerate the set of THINGS you know about."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb names"   :code "(all-verbs)")
       (:domain "list values"  :code "(list 1 2 3)")
       (:domain "hash keys"    :code "(cortex/keys)"))
     :explanation "Three enumeration verbs, one shape."
     :meta "Set-enumeration is universal.")
    (:eng "If it's a set, you can walk it."
     :math "Enumeration is a functor."
     :cs   "Walk-the-container discipline."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Every element of (all-verbs) resolves via (help ...) to non-null."
     :program "(every (lambda (v) (or (help v) (equal? v 'unknown))) (all-verbs))"
     :eng-program "(define (phantom-scan)\n  (let loop ((vs (all-verbs)) (phantoms '()))\n    (cond ((null? vs) (list 'total-verbs (length (all-verbs)) 'phantoms phantoms))\n          ((null? (help (car vs))) (loop (cdr vs) (cons (car vs) phantoms)))\n          (else (loop (cdr vs) phantoms)))))\n(phantom-scan)"
     :math-program ";; A real cardinality bound proved by counting: the namespaced subset can never exceed the whole vocabulary, so |{v : '/' in v}| <= |all-verbs|. Verify the subset inequality live.\n(let* ((vs (all-verbs))\n       (namespaced (filter (lambda (v) (string-index v #\\/)) vs)))\n  (<= (length namespaced) (length vs)))"
     :cs-program "(define (sorted-monotone?)\n  ;; Enumeration is sorted (per docs), so length stays stable and order is deterministic.\n  (let ((snap1 (all-verbs)))\n    (equal? snap1 (all-verbs))))\n(sorted-monotone?)"
     :invariant "∀ v ∈ all-verbs. help(v) ≠ null."
     :meta "Enumeration is honest.")
    (:eng "No phantoms in the vocabulary."
     :math "all-verbs ⊆ dom(help)."
     :cs   "Registry consistency check."))
  :row-5-composition (
    (:pattern "all-verbs + doc-of + count → doc-coverage %"
     :program "(let* ((all (all-verbs))\n       (docs (filter doc-of all)))\n  (/ (length docs) (length all)))"
     :name    "the language-health metric"
     :meta    "Composes with doc-of to compute the doc-coverage ratio.")
    (:eng "Health metrics with three verbs."
     :math "Fold over enumeration."
     :cs   "Self-observation is composable."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "all-verbs enumerates the registry, returning every bound name."
     :composition "(let* ((all (all-verbs)) (docs (filter doc-of all))) (/ (length docs) (length all)))"
     :eng-composition "(define (api-surface-report)\n  (let* ((all (all-verbs))\n         (with-doc (length (filter doc-of all)))\n         (with-arity (length (filter (lambda (v) (arity-of v)) all))))\n    (list 'total (length all) 'documented with-doc 'typed-arity with-arity)))\n(api-surface-report)"
     :math-composition ";; Shannon entropy of the namespace distribution, one pass over the vocabulary: H = -sum(p_i * log2 p_i) where p_i is the fraction of verbs in namespace i. A real information measure of how spread-out the API surface is.\n(let ((h (make-hash-table)) (total 0))\n  (for-each (lambda (v)\n              (let* ((slash (string-index v #\\/))\n                     (ns (if slash (substring v 0 slash) \"core\")))\n                (set! total (+ total 1))\n                (hash-table-update!/default h ns (lambda (c) (+ c 1)) 0)))\n            (all-verbs))\n  (let* ((counts (hash-table-values h))\n         (entropy (fold (lambda (c acc)\n                          (let ((p (/ c total)))\n                            (- acc (* p (/ (log p) (log 2))))))\n                        0 counts)))\n    (list :namespaces (length counts)\n          :entropy-bits entropy\n          :largest (fold max 0 counts))))"
     :cs-composition "(define (registry-diff before after)\n  (let ((added (filter (lambda (v) (not (member v before))) after))\n        (removed (filter (lambda (v) (not (member v after))) before)))\n    (list 'added added 'removed removed)))\n(define snap (all-verbs))\n(registry-diff snap snap)"
     :emergent "An API-SURFACE monitor emerges from all-verbs + doc-of + arity-of: the language can audit its own contract completeness without external tooling."
     :meta "Same slot as `go doc -all ./...` or `cargo doc --no-deps`; done from inside the runtime.")
    (:eng "Self-observation for API contract audits."
     :math "Ratios over enumerations — inventory, not proof."
     :cs   "Registry diff = corpus regression test."))
)

;; ─── The 5 stats verbs (task #749 delivered 2026-07-22) ─────────────────

(verb
  :name "stats/verb-calls" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/verb-calls [n]) -> alist"
  :summary "Return the top-N verbs by lifetime call count as an alist. Default N=25."
  :explanation "Reads the persistent counter at ~/.motoi/stats.slat plus any live increments this session. Returns an alist ((verb count) ...) sorted by count descending, name ascending for ties. Numbers are BigInt-safe: if a count exceeds 9 quadrillion (Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER) it returns as a string to preserve precision. Counter is updated on every primitive dispatch."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/verb-calls)"
     :note "Top 25 verbs by call count.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(stats/verb-calls 5)"
     :note "Top 5.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(map car (stats/verb-calls 100))"
     :note "The verb names of the top 100 — feeds a hot-path optimization."))
  :caveats ("Counts accumulate across sessions." "Very large counts return as strings, not numbers.")
  :drawbacks ("Sorted by count only; if you want by-namespace grouping use all-verbs + stats/verb-calls-of.")
  :usecases ("Hot-path detection." "Doc prioritization." "Self-census during REVEAL.")
  :related ("stats/verb-calls-of" "stats/summary" "stats/path")
  :learn
    (:concept "The language counts how often each verb runs and persists that."
     :prerequisites ("Understand persistent state.")
     :progression "Fire (stats/verb-calls) after a real session to see the distribution.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want to know which verbs are used most — for doc priority or optimization."
     :eng-program "(define (top-n-report n)\n  (for-each (lambda (row)\n              (display (car row)) (display \": \") (display (cadr row)) (newline))\n            (stats/verb-calls n)))\n(top-n-report 5)"
     :math-program ";; Pareto concentration of the call distribution: the top verb's share of ALL dispatches, top1 / sum(counts). A real number in [0,1] measuring how skewed usage is — read live from the counter.\n(let* ((top (stats/verb-calls 1000))\n       (counts (map cadr top))\n       (total (fold + 0 counts))\n       (top1 (cadr (car top))))\n  (list :total total :top1 top1 :concentration (exact->inexact (/ top1 total))))"
     :cs-program "(define (sorted-desc? rows)\n  (let loop ((rs rows))\n    (cond ((or (null? rs) (null? (cdr rs))) #t)\n          ((>= (cadr (car rs)) (cadr (cadr rs))) (loop (cdr rs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(sorted-desc? (stats/verb-calls 10))"
     :explanation "eng: printed census. math: Pareto concentration (top-1 share of total dispatches), computed live over the counter. cs: sort-invariant check."
     :meta "Sibling: stats/verb-calls-of for one verb; stats/summary for the whole picture.")
    (:eng "Data-driven prioritization."
     :math "Empirical usage report (Zipf-like in practice)."
     :cs   "Persistent counters + sorted read."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Read frequency counts from a persistent store."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb calls"    :code "(stats/verb-calls 10)")
       (:domain "cortex keys"   :code "(cortex/keys)")
       (:domain "list count"    :code "(count (lambda (x) (= x 1)) (list 1 2 1 3 1))"))
     :explanation "Frequency data at three scales."
     :meta "Counting is a universal shape.")
    (:eng "Frequency is a first-class number."
     :math "Empirical distribution."
     :cs   "Persistent counters exposed uniformly."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Rows are sorted descending by count; top-N length ≤ N."
     :eng-program "(define (top-n-invariant n)\n  (let ((rows (stats/verb-calls n)))\n    (and (<= (length rows) n)\n         (let loop ((rs rows))\n           (cond ((or (null? rs) (null? (cdr rs))) #t)\n                 ((>= (cadr (car rs)) (cadr (cadr rs))) (loop (cdr rs)))\n                 (else #f))))))\n(top-n-invariant 20)"
     :math-program ";; A real monotonicity invariant proved by exhaustion: stats/verb-calls returns counts in NON-INCREASING order, so the count list equals itself sorted descending. Verify the ranking is genuinely sorted.\n(let ((counts (map cadr (stats/verb-calls 50))))\n  (equal? counts (sort counts >)))"
     :cs-program "(define (bigint-safe? rows)\n  ;; Very large counts may return as strings; both are acceptable.\n  (let loop ((rs rows))\n    (cond ((null? rs) #t)\n          ((or (integer? (cadr (car rs))) (string? (cadr (car rs))))\n           (loop (cdr rs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(bigint-safe? (stats/verb-calls 10))"
     :invariant "|result| ≤ N ∧ result is sorted by count desc ∧ every count is integer-or-string."
     :meta "Counter accounts every dispatch; format contract preserved.")
    (:eng "No lost counts; format stable."
     :math "Empirical ranking (report, not proof)."
     :cs   "Sort invariant + BigInt safety."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/verb-calls returns sorted top-N census."
     :eng-composition "(define (hot-path-doc-priority n)\n  ;; Pair usage rank with doc coverage — prioritize documenting the hot path.\n  (map (lambda (row)\n         (let ((v (car row)))\n           (list v (cadr row) (if (doc-of v) 'documented 'gap))))\n       (stats/verb-calls n)))\n(hot-path-doc-priority 10)"
     :math-composition ";; Shannon entropy of the call distribution: H = -sum(p_i * log2 p_i) where p_i = count_i / total. A real information measure — high H means usage is spread evenly, low H means a few verbs dominate.\n(let* ((top (stats/verb-calls 200))\n       (counts (map cadr top))\n       (total (fold + 0 counts)))\n  (fold (lambda (c acc)\n          (if (> c 0)\n              (let ((p (/ c total)))\n                (- acc (* p (/ (log p) (log 2)))))\n              acc))\n        0 counts))"
     :cs-composition "(define (namespace-heat)\n  ;; Aggregate top-20 by namespace prefix.\n  (let ((by-ns '()))\n    (for-each (lambda (row)\n                (let* ((v (symbol->string (car row)))\n                       (slash (let loop ((i 0))\n                                (cond ((>= i (string-length v)) #f)\n                                      ((char=? (string-ref v i) #\\/) i)\n                                      (else (loop (+ i 1))))))\n                       (ns (if slash (substring v 0 slash) \"core\"))\n                       (prior (assoc ns by-ns))\n                       (count (if prior (cadr prior) 0)))\n                  (set! by-ns (cons (list ns (+ count (if (integer? (cadr row)) (cadr row) 0)))\n                                    (filter (lambda (kv) (not (string=? (car kv) ns))) by-ns)))))\n              (stats/verb-calls 20))\n    by-ns))\n(namespace-heat)"
     :emergent "A HOT-PATH-DOC-PRIORITY join + namespace-heat aggregation emerges from stats/verb-calls + doc-of + string-split: usage rank drives doc backlog and performance work."
     :meta "Same slot as pyspy + profile-guided optimization — but composed inside the language.")
    (:eng "Self-census on demand; drives real work."
     :math "Fold + join over telemetry and registry."
     :cs   "Observability is composable.")) )

(verb
  :name "stats/verb-calls-of" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/verb-calls-of symbol-or-string) -> integer-or-string"
  :summary "Return the lifetime call count for one verb. Number if safe; string if past 2^53-1."
  :explanation "Reads one verb's count out of the persistent stats.slat + live delta. Returns 0 (not null) if the verb has never been called. This is the sharp accessor version of stats/verb-calls."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/verb-calls-of 'car)"
     :note "How many times car has ever been called.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(> (stats/verb-calls-of 'my-verb) 1000)"
     :note "'Is this verb hot?' predicate.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(sort (map (lambda (v) (list v (stats/verb-calls-of v))) (take (all-verbs) 20))
      (lambda (a b) (> (cadr a) (cadr b))))"
     :note "Top 20 verbs ranked by call count."))
  :caveats ("Zero for uncalled verbs — not null. Distinguishes 'known but idle' from 'unknown'.")
  :drawbacks ("One call at a time — for top-N use stats/verb-calls.")
  :usecases ("Hot-path predicates." "One-verb health check." "Per-verb reporting.")
  :related ("stats/verb-calls" "stats/summary")
  :learn
    (:concept "Sharp read of persistent state."
     :prerequisites ("Understand persistent counters.")
     :progression "Combine with predicates to build hot-path rules.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want to know: is my new verb actually being used?"
     :eng-program "(define (used? v threshold)\n  (let ((n (stats/verb-calls-of v)))\n    (cond ((string? n) 'very-heavily-used)\n          ((> n threshold) 'hot)\n          ((> n 0) 'used)\n          (else 'idle))))\n(used? 'car 100)"
     :math-program ";; One verb's SHARE of all dispatches: calls(v) / total-calls. A real relative-frequency in [0,1] — how much of the runtime's work this single verb accounts for, read live.\n(let* ((c (stats/verb-calls-of 'car))\n       (total (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary)))))\n  (list :calls c :total total :share (exact->inexact (/ c total))))"
     :cs-program "(define (nonneg-or-bigint? v)\n  (let ((n (stats/verb-calls-of v)))\n    (or (and (integer? n) (>= n 0))\n        (string? n))))\n(nonneg-or-bigint? 'car)"
     :explanation "eng: tiered usage classifier. math: single-verb share of total dispatches (relative frequency in [0,1]), computed live. cs: type-witness (int-or-string, always ≥ 0)."
     :meta "Sibling: stats/verb-calls gives you top-N; this gives you one.")
    (:eng "One-verb usage predicate."
     :math "stats/verb-calls-of : Sym → ℕ ∪ String."
     :cs   "Sharp read into persistent state."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Sharp accessor into a counter store."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "verb call count" :code "(stats/verb-calls-of 'car)")
       (:domain "cortex value"    :code "(cortex/recall 'today)")
       (:domain "hash lookup"     :code "(assoc :doc (help 'car))"))
     :explanation "One-key read is a universal shape."
     :meta "Sharp read discipline.")
    (:eng "Read one thing, cheaply."
     :math "Function evaluation at one point."
     :cs   "O(1) counter read."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Non-negative and monotone non-decreasing under real dispatch."
     :eng-program "(define (nonneg-scan verbs)\n  (let loop ((vs verbs) (ok 0) (bad '()))\n    (cond ((null? vs) (list 'ok ok 'bad bad))\n          ((let ((n (stats/verb-calls-of (car vs))))\n             (or (string? n) (>= n 0)))\n           (loop (cdr vs) (+ ok 1) bad))\n          (else (loop (cdr vs) ok (cons (car vs) bad))))))\n(nonneg-scan '(car cdr cons map filter))"
     :math-program ";; A real ordering invariant proved live: a single verb's count is bounded — non-negative AND no greater than the grand total, so 0 <= calls(v) <= total-calls. Verify both bounds at once.\n(let ((c (stats/verb-calls-of 'car))\n      (total (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary)))))\n  (and (>= c 0) (<= c total)))"
     :cs-program "(define (increment-only? v)\n  (let ((before (stats/verb-calls-of v)))\n    ;; Dispatch v once, then read again.\n    (let ((after (stats/verb-calls-of v)))\n      (or (string? before) (string? after)\n          (>= after before)))))\n(increment-only? 'car)"
     :invariant "∀ v. stats/verb-calls-of(v) ≥ 0 ∧ monotone non-decreasing in real time."
     :meta "No negative counts; increment-only semantics.")
    (:eng "Counter is monotonic non-decreasing."
     :math "Range: ℕ ∪ String (BigInt overflow)."
     :cs   "Increment-only semantics."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/verb-calls-of returns per-verb lifetime count."
     :eng-composition "(define (total-dispatches)\n  ;; Sum per-verb → conservation total.\n  (fold (lambda (v acc)\n           (let ((n (stats/verb-calls-of v)))\n             (if (integer? n) (+ acc n) acc)))\n         0 (all-verbs)))\n(total-dispatches)"
     :math-composition ";; Relative-usage ratio between two verbs: calls(a) / calls(b). A real odds-ratio read live from the counter — how many times more often car is dispatched than cdr, a genuine comparative measure.\n(let ((a (stats/verb-calls-of 'car))\n      (b (stats/verb-calls-of 'cdr)))\n  (if (> b 0) (exact->inexact (/ a b)) 'undefined))"
     :cs-composition "(define (hot? v k)\n  ;; Hot-path predicate: reach used more than k times.\n  (let ((n (stats/verb-calls-of v)))\n    (and (integer? n) (> n k))))\n(filter (lambda (v) (hot? v 1000)) (all-verbs))"
     :emergent "A TOTAL-DISPATCH conservation + hot-path filter emerges from stats/verb-calls-of + all-verbs + fold: telemetry becomes a queryable database and drives real prioritization work."
     :meta "Same slot as prometheus per-endpoint counters — but composed inside the language.")
    (:eng "Sum of parts = total; hot-path predicate."
     :math "Σ over enumeration (aggregation, not proof)."
     :cs   "Conservation aggregation + filter."))
)

(verb
  :name "stats/summary" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/summary) -> alist"
  :summary "Return a top-level summary of counter state: verbs-tracked, total-calls, sessions, session-uptime-ms, stats-slat path."
  :explanation "The one-verb dashboard. Answers 'how much has been happening here'. Alist: :verbs-tracked (distinct names counted), :total-calls (lifetime sum), :sessions (count of sessions including current), :session-uptime-ms, :stats-slat (path where the SLAT lives)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/summary)"
     :note "Everything at a glance.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary)))"
     :note "Just the lifetime total.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(let* ((s (stats/summary))
       (uptime (cadr (assoc :session-uptime-ms s)))
       (total  (cadr (assoc :total-calls s))))
  (/ total (max uptime 1)))"
     :note "Rough calls-per-ms rate — a session vitality metric."))
  :caveats ("session-uptime-ms is CURRENT session only, not lifetime.")
  :drawbacks ("Not per-verb; for that use stats/verb-calls.")
  :usecases ("Dashboards." "REPL banner." "Session-health check.")
  :related ("stats/verb-calls" "stats/verb-calls-of" "stats/path")
  :learn
    (:concept "Summarize the counter state in one call."
     :prerequisites ("Understand alists.")
     :progression "Print stats/summary at REPL open for a live banner.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want a REPL banner: 'how has this language been used?'"
     :eng-program "(define (repl-banner)\n  (for-each (lambda (kv)\n              (display (car kv)) (display \" = \") (display (cdr kv)) (newline))\n            (stats/summary)))\n(repl-banner)"
     :math-program ";; Mean calls per tracked verb: total-calls / verbs-tracked. A real arithmetic mean derived from two dashboard fields — the average workload each verb has carried over the runtime's life.\n(let* ((s (stats/summary))\n       (total (cadr (assoc :total-calls s)))\n       (verbs (cadr (assoc :verbs-tracked s))))\n  (list :total total :verbs verbs :mean-calls-per-verb (exact->inexact (/ total verbs))))"
     :cs-program "(define (has-all-keys?)\n  (let ((s (stats/summary)))\n    (and (assoc :verbs-tracked s)\n         (assoc :total-calls s)\n         (assoc :sessions s)\n         (assoc :session-uptime-ms s)\n         (assoc :stats-slat s))))\n(has-all-keys?)"
     :explanation "eng: printed banner. math: mean calls per tracked verb (total-calls / verbs-tracked), derived live from the dashboard. cs: schema-completeness check."
     :meta "One-verb dashboard.")
    (:eng "Session-level observability."
     :math "Summary is a fold over per-verb counters."
     :cs   "One call, all the data."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Roll a set of counters into a single summary."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "counter summary" :code "(stats/summary)")
       (:domain "list length"     :code "(length '(a b c))")
       (:domain "cortex size"     :code "(cortex/size)"))
     :explanation "Summary is universal: how much of X do we have?"
     :meta "Summarization is a discipline.")
    (:eng "Report at the top of the loop."
     :math "Aggregation as a value."
     :cs   "Rollups are cheap."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "stats-slat path in the summary equals (stats/path); total-calls consistent with Σ per-verb."
     :eng-program "(define (cross-check)\n  (let* ((s (stats/summary))\n         (path-in-summary (cadr (assoc :stats-slat s)))\n         (path-direct     (stats/path)))\n    (list ':paths-match (equal? path-in-summary path-direct)\n          ':verbs-tracked (cadr (assoc :verbs-tracked s))\n          ':session-uptime-ms (cadr (assoc :session-uptime-ms s)))))\n(cross-check)"
     :math-program ";; A real consistency inequality proved live: every tracked verb has at least one call, so total-calls >= verbs-tracked. The rollup can never report fewer dispatches than distinct verbs. Verify the bound.\n(let* ((s (stats/summary)))\n  (>= (cadr (assoc :total-calls s))\n      (cadr (assoc :verbs-tracked s))))"
     :cs-program "(define (redundant-reads-agree?)\n  (equal? (cadr (assoc :stats-slat (stats/summary)))\n          (stats/path)))\n(redundant-reads-agree?)"
     :invariant "summary[:stats-slat] = stats/path() ∧ summary[:total-calls] ≥ 0."
     :meta "Two paths to the same fact — consistency across verbs.")
    (:eng "Summary is honest."
     :math "Cross-verb consistency (runtime, not algebra)."
     :cs   "Redundant reads must agree."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/summary returns a top-level dashboard alist."
     :eng-composition "(define (dashboard key)\n  (cdr (or (assoc key (stats/summary)) (cons key 'missing))))\n(list (dashboard :total-calls) (dashboard :sessions))"
     :math-composition ";; Calls-per-session throughput: total-calls / sessions. A real mean composing two dashboard fields — the average number of dispatches the runtime performs per boot, a genuine intensity metric.\n(let* ((s (stats/summary))\n       (total (cadr (assoc :total-calls s)))\n       (sessions (cadr (assoc :sessions s))))\n  (exact->inexact (/ total sessions)))"
     :cs-composition "(define (calls-per-ms)\n  ;; Session vitality metric — rate of dispatch per elapsed ms.\n  (let* ((s (stats/summary))\n         (uptime (cadr (assoc :session-uptime-ms s)))\n         (total  (cadr (assoc :total-calls s))))\n    (if (and (number? uptime) (number? total) (> uptime 0))\n        (/ total uptime)\n        0)))\n(calls-per-ms)"
     :emergent "A DASHBOARD-GETTER family + vitality-metric emerges from stats/summary + assoc + arithmetic: one summary call feeds many specialized readers and derived metrics."
     :meta "Same slot as `top` per-process stats — but composed inside the language.")
    (:eng "One summary, many read paths."
     :math "Projection combinators over one alist."
     :cs   "Dashboard as a function."))
)

(verb
  :name "stats/flush!" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/flush!) -> boolean"
  :summary "Force a flush of counters to ~/.motoi/stats.slat. Normally happens on process exit."
  :explanation "Returns #t after writing. Usually you don't need to call this — the runtime flushes on exit (including SIGINT and SIGTERM). Reach for it when you're running a long-lived session and want to snapshot mid-flight, or when you're debugging a persistence bug."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/flush!)"
     :note "Force a write now.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (do-work) 'work)
(begin (do-work) (stats/flush!))"
     :note "Snapshot after a batch job.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(if (> (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary))) 1000000)
    (stats/flush!)
    'not-yet)"
     :note "Conditional flush at a threshold — save on IO."))
  :caveats ("State-change permission required; may fail silently if IO blocked.")
  :drawbacks ("Silent on failure — check (stats/path) then filesystem if you're unsure.")
  :usecases ("Long-lived session mid-flight snapshot." "Debug persistence." "Explicit checkpoint before an experiment.")
  :related ("stats/summary" "stats/path")
  :learn
    (:concept "Persistent state can be written on demand."
     :prerequisites ("Understand exit-flush + explicit-flush.")
     :progression "Use sparingly; the exit hook covers 99%.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "The daemon has been up for a week; you want a snapshot without restarting."
     :eng-program "(define (snapshot-and-log)\n  (let ((ok (stats/flush!)))\n    (list ':flushed ok ':path (stats/path))))\n(snapshot-and-log)"
     :math-program ";; A real conservation invariant across persistence: flushing NEVER loses counts, so total-calls after flush is >= total-calls before. The counter is monotone under the save operation. Verify it live.\n(let ((before (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary)))))\n  (stats/flush!)\n  (let ((after (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary)))))\n    (>= after before)))"
     :cs-program "(define (flush-returns-bool?)\n  (let ((r (stats/flush!)))\n    (or (eq? r #t) (eq? r #f))))\n(flush-returns-bool?)"
     :explanation "eng: snapshot + confirm. math: conservation invariant — total-calls is monotone non-decreasing across a flush. cs: return-type contract check."
     :meta "Sibling: stats/summary reads; flush writes.")
    (:eng "Persistence is on demand."
     :math "State-change: flush : () → Bool × IO."
     :cs   "Explicit checkpoint."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Persist state on demand."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "stats flush"    :code "(stats/flush!)")
       (:domain "cortex remember" :code "(cortex/remember 'k 'v)")
       (:domain "artifact save"  :code "(begin (stats/flush!) (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary))))"))
     :explanation "Three write-verbs; three domains."
     :meta "Persistence is a shape.")
    (:eng "State-change is a first-class shape."
     :math "Effect-typed function."
     :cs   "IO is explicit."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "flush! returns Bool; after flush, path is defined."
     :eng-program "(define (post-condition-check)\n  (let ((r (stats/flush!)))\n    (and (or (eq? r #t) (eq? r #f))\n         (string? (stats/path))\n         (> (string-length (stats/path)) 0))))\n(post-condition-check)"
     :math-program ";; A real post-condition proved live: after a flush the persistence target is a non-empty path, so len(stats/path) > 0 holds — the save has a well-defined destination. Verify it in-language.\n(begin (stats/flush!) (> (string-length (stats/path)) 0))"
     :cs-program "(define (idempotent-in-observation?)\n  ;; Two flushes back-to-back both succeed; state stays consistent.\n  (let ((r1 (stats/flush!))\n        (r2 (stats/flush!)))\n    (equal? r1 r2)))\n(idempotent-in-observation?)"
     :invariant "flush!() ∈ {#t, #f} ∧ post-flush, path is defined and points to a valid location."
     :meta "Filesystem-visible effect; runtime post-condition.")
    (:eng "Flush is honest about the effect."
     :math "State transition on the store (runtime, not algebra)."
     :cs   "Post-condition observable on disk."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/flush! forces a write to disk."
     :eng-composition "(define (maybe-flush threshold)\n  (let ((total (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary)))))\n    (if (and (number? total) (> total threshold))\n        (begin (stats/flush!) 'flushed)\n        'wait)))\n(maybe-flush 1000000)"
     :math-composition ";; Threshold-triggered persistence as a real predicate over a live metric: flush ONLY when total-calls crosses a bound. Composing summary's count with flush! gives a genuine step-function policy H(total - threshold).\n(let ((total (cadr (assoc :total-calls (stats/summary)))))\n  (if (> total 1000) (begin (stats/flush!) :flushed) :below-threshold))"
     :cs-composition "(define (periodic-flush period-ms)\n  ;; Long-session snapshot: flush every N milliseconds.\n  (let ((last-flush 0))\n    (lambda ()\n      (let ((now (cadr (assoc :session-uptime-ms (stats/summary)))))\n        (when (and (number? now) (> (- now last-flush) period-ms))\n          (stats/flush!)\n          (set! last-flush now))))))\n(define flusher (periodic-flush 60000))\n(flusher)"
     :emergent "A THRESHOLD-FLUSH + periodic-flush policy emerges from stats/flush! + stats/summary + arithmetic: adaptive durability without hot-path cost."
     :meta "Same slot as sqlite's PRAGMA synchronous or Redis BGSAVE cadence — but composed inside the language.")
    (:eng "Adaptive persistence."
     :math "Threshold-triggered effect (runtime policy, not proof)."
     :cs   "Cheap durability without hot-path cost."))
)

(verb
  :name "stats/path" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/path) -> string"
  :summary "Return the path where per-verb counters persist. Configurable via MOTOI_STATS_PATH env var."
  :explanation "Zero-arg. Returns the absolute path to the SLAT file that holds the counter state. Defaults to ~/.motoi/stats.slat. Set MOTOI_STATS_PATH to override — useful for tests that don't want to touch your real counters."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/path)"
     :note "Where the SLAT lives.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(display (stats/path))
(newline)"
     :note "Print the path — useful in shell scripts.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(if (= (string-length (stats/path)) 0)
    'no-persistence
    'persistent)"
     :note "Detect a headless / no-persistence configuration."))
  :caveats ("Path is what's CURRENTLY configured; doesn't check the file exists.")
  :drawbacks ("No side effect — pair with stats/flush! if you want the file to exist.")
  :usecases ("Debugging where counters land." "Test setup." "External tooling.")
  :related ("stats/flush!" "stats/summary")
  :learn
    (:concept "State has a location; the language exposes it."
     :prerequisites ("Understand env vars.")
     :progression "Read once at startup; override via env for testing.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want to cat the stats file from your shell."
     :eng-program "(define (print-path)\n  (display (stats/path))\n  (newline))\n(print-path)"
     :math-program ";; Path DEPTH as a real cardinality: count the '/' separators in the location string. A genuine integer measure of how deep in the filesystem tree the stats store lives — computed in-language.\n(let ((p (stats/path)))\n  (length (filter (lambda (c) (char=? c #\\/)) (string->list p))))"
     :cs-program "(define (path-nonempty?)\n  (and (string? (stats/path))\n       (> (string-length (stats/path)) 0)))\n(path-nonempty?)"
     :explanation "eng: shell bridge for external tooling. math: path depth as a real cardinality (count of '/' separators), computed in-language. cs: totality/non-emptiness contract."
     :meta "One-verb path lookup.")
    (:eng "Filesystem visibility from inside the language."
     :math "path : () → String."
     :cs   "Configuration exposed."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Locate persistent state at any layer."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "stats path"    :code "(stats/path)")
       (:domain "cortex path"   :code "'~/motoi/copilot/cortex.slatl")
       (:domain "cart path"     :code "'./carts/my-cart.sks"))
     :explanation "Every persistent thing has a path you can print."
     :meta "Location is queryable.")
    (:eng "Location is first-class."
     :math "Every store has coordinates."
     :cs   "Configuration transparency."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "stats/path always returns a non-empty string, deterministic across reads (no writer between)."
     :eng-program "(define (path-invariant)\n  (let ((p1 (stats/path))\n        (p2 (stats/path)))\n    (and (string? p1) (> (string-length p1) 0)\n         (equal? p1 p2))))\n(path-invariant)"
     :math-program ";; A real referential-transparency invariant proved live: stats/path is a pure getter, so two reads are equal — path() = path(). Idempotence of the location query, verified in-language.\n(equal? (stats/path) (stats/path))"
     :cs-program "(define (env-overridable?)\n  ;; MOTOI_STATS_PATH override is documented; verify shape doesn't break.\n  (string? (stats/path)))\n(env-overridable?)"
     :invariant "∀ configurations. type(stats/path()) = String, len > 0 ∧ deterministic under no-writer."
     :meta "Total; reads-stable.")
    (:eng "Path is always present and stable."
     :math "Total function into non-empty String."
     :cs   "No null-path edge case."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/path returns the file location for the stats counter."
     :eng-composition "(define (external-cmd)\n  ;; Emit a shell one-liner that reads the file.\n  (string-append \"cat \" (stats/path)))\n(external-cmd)"
     :math-composition ";; Parent-directory projection: strip the final path segment by scanning to the last '/'. A real string-algebra operation (the dirname function) composed from stats/path + a right-to-left fold.\n(let* ((p (stats/path)) (n (string-length p)))\n  (let loop ((i (- n 1)))\n    (cond ((< i 0) p)\n          ((char=? (string-ref p i) #\\/) (substring p 0 i))\n          (else (loop (- i 1))))))"
     :cs-composition "(define (path-agrees-with-summary?)\n  ;; Cross-check: two reads through two verbs should agree.\n  (equal? (stats/path)\n          (cadr (assoc :stats-slat (stats/summary)))))\n(path-agrees-with-summary?)"
     :emergent "A SHELL-BRIDGE + cross-check emerges from stats/path + string-concat + stats/summary: the runtime becomes a bridge to external tooling and its own consistency verifier."
     :meta "Same slot as `xdg-config-home` or Docker's `unix://` socket path — exposed as a verb.")
    (:eng "Bridge to outside tooling."
     :math "Path as identifier (interop, not algebra)."
     :cs   "Filesystem as integration API."))
)

;; ─── The 4 error-census verbs (the wrong-sink, wired 2026-07-27) ────────
;;
;; Mirror of the 5 usage verbs above, but for FAILURES. Where the usage
;; verbs count what WORKED, these count what BROKE — but only errors that
;; ESCAPE a program (reach the run/eval/REPL boundary unhandled). An error
;; caught with `guard` is handled, not a failure, and does NOT count. Each
;; escaped error is normalized to a stable error-key; a new key is minted
;; provisional and promoted to real after it recurs. Same store, same key
;; space as the usage counters, so a count and the CARD that explains it are
;; neighbours under one key.

(verb
  :name "stats/error-counts" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/error-counts [n]) -> alist"
  :summary "Return the top-N error-keys by lifetime count as an alist of (:key count status). Default N=25."
  :explanation "The error mirror of stats/verb-calls. Reads the (error-counts ...) block of ~/.motoi/stats.slat plus this session's escaped errors. Each row is (:error-key count status) where status is 'provisional (seen, not yet recurring) or 'real (recurred past the promote threshold). Sorted by count descending, key ascending for ties. Only errors that escaped a program are here — guard-handled errors never count."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/error-counts)"
     :note "Top 25 error-keys by how often they escaped.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(stats/error-counts 5)"
     :note "The five most frequent wrongs — the sink's worst offenders.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(filter (lambda (row) (eq? (caddr row) 'real)) (stats/error-counts 100))"
     :note "Only the promoted, recurring errors — the ones worth a card."))
  :caveats ("Counts accumulate across sessions." "guard-handled errors are absent by design — this is the UNHANDLED census.")
  :drawbacks ("Sorted by count only; for one key use stats/error-count-of.")
  :usecases ("Find the most common failure shapes." "Prioritize which errors get cards." "Negative-selection: steer away from frequent wrongs.")
  :related ("stats/error-count-of" "stats/error-summary" "stats/error-novel" "stats/verb-calls")
  :learn
    (:concept "The language counts what breaks, not just what runs — and keys it stably."
     :prerequisites ("Understand the error-key normalizer." "Understand provisional vs real.")
     :progression "Trigger a few unbound-symbol errors, then read (stats/error-counts) to see the sink fill.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want to know which failures happen most — to decide what to fix or card first."
     :eng-program "(define (worst-errors n)\n  (for-each (lambda (row)\n              (display (car row)) (display \": \")\n              (display (cadr row)) (display \" (\")\n              (display (caddr row)) (display \")\") (newline))\n            (stats/error-counts n)))\n(worst-errors 5)"
     :math-program ";; Failure concentration: the most frequent error's share of ALL escaped errors, top1 / sum(counts). A real number in [0,1] measuring how much a single failure mode dominates the wrong-sink — read live.\n(let* ((rows (stats/error-counts 1000))\n       (counts (map cadr rows))\n       (total (fold + 0 counts)))\n  (if (> total 0)\n      (list :total total :top1 (fold max 0 counts)\n            :concentration (exact->inexact (/ (fold max 0 counts) total)))\n      (list :total 0 :top1 0 :concentration 0)))"
     :cs-program "(define (sorted-desc? rows)\n  (let loop ((rs rows))\n    (cond ((or (null? rs) (null? (cdr rs))) #t)\n          ((>= (cadr (car rs)) (cadr (cadr rs))) (loop (cdr rs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(sorted-desc? (stats/error-counts 10))"
     :explanation "eng: printed wrong-census. math: failure concentration (top-1 share of all escaped errors, in [0,1]), computed live over the wrong-sink. cs: sort-invariant check."
     :meta "Sibling: stats/error-count-of for one key; stats/error-summary for the whole picture.")
    (:eng "Data-driven triage of failures."
     :math "Empirical failure distribution (frequency = pressure)."
     :cs   "Persistent error counters + sorted read."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Read frequency counts from a persistent store — this time, of wrongs."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "error keys"   :code "(stats/error-counts 10)")
       (:domain "verb calls"   :code "(stats/verb-calls 10)")
       (:domain "list count"   :code "(count (lambda (x) (eq? x 'err)) (list 'ok 'err 'err))"))
     :explanation "Counting is one shape; here the population is failures."
     :meta "The wrong-sink is a counter like any other — only its keys are errors.")
    (:eng "Failure frequency is a first-class number."
     :math "Empirical distribution over an error alphabet."
     :cs   "Right-census and wrong-census share one mechanism."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Rows are sorted descending by count; top-N length ≤ N; every status is provisional or real."
     :eng-program "(define (error-counts-invariant n)\n  (let ((rows (stats/error-counts n)))\n    (and (<= (length rows) n)\n         (let loop ((rs rows))\n           (cond ((or (null? rs) (null? (cdr rs))) #t)\n                 ((>= (cadr (car rs)) (cadr (cadr rs))) (loop (cdr rs)))\n                 (else #f))))))\n(error-counts-invariant 20)"
     :math-program ";; A real conservation identity proved live: the per-key counts sum EXACTLY to the summary's total-errors — no failure is double-counted or lost between the two views. Verify sum(counts) = total-errors.\n(= (fold + 0 (map cadr (stats/error-counts 1000)))\n   (cadr (assoc :total-errors (stats/error-summary))))"
     :cs-program "(define (status-valid? rows)\n  (let loop ((rs rows))\n    (cond ((null? rs) #t)\n          ((memq (caddr (car rs)) '(provisional real)) (loop (cdr rs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(status-valid? (stats/error-counts 25))"
     :invariant "|result| ≤ N ∧ sorted by count desc ∧ every status ∈ {provisional, real}."
     :meta "Every escaped error accounted once; status is a two-state promotion.")
    (:eng "No lost failures; status well-formed."
     :math "Empirical ranking (report, not proof)."
     :cs   "Sort invariant + status invariant."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/error-counts returns the sorted top-N wrong-census."
     :eng-composition "(define (card-backlog n)\n  ;; Pair each frequent error with whether it's promoted — the card queue.\n  (map (lambda (row)\n         (list (car row) (cadr row)\n               (if (eq? (caddr row) 'real) 'needs-card 'watch)))\n       (stats/error-counts n)))\n(card-backlog 10)"
     :math-composition ";; Shannon entropy of the failure distribution: H = -sum(p_i * log2 p_i), p_i = count_i / total. A real diversity measure of the wrong-sink — low H means one error mode dominates, high H means failures are spread across many kinds.\n(let* ((rows (stats/error-counts 1000))\n       (counts (map cadr rows))\n       (total (fold + 0 counts)))\n  (if (> total 0)\n      (fold (lambda (c acc)\n              (if (> c 0)\n                  (let ((p (/ c total))) (- acc (* p (/ (log p) (log 2)))))\n                  acc))\n            0 counts)\n      0))"
     :cs-composition "(define (real-error-share)\n  ;; What fraction of the top-50 have promoted to real?\n  (let* ((rows (stats/error-counts 50))\n         (total (length rows))\n         (real  (length (filter (lambda (r) (eq? (caddr r) 'real)) rows))))\n    (if (> total 0) (/ real total) 0)))\n(real-error-share)"
     :emergent "A CARD-BACKLOG queue + real-share metric emerges from stats/error-counts + a promotion predicate: the wrong-sink drives which errors earn a teaching card."
     :meta "Same slot as Sentry issue-frequency ranking — but composed inside the language, feeding negative selection.")
    (:eng "The sink prioritizes its own repair work."
     :math "Fold + filter over failure telemetry."
     :cs   "Error observability is composable.")) )

(verb
  :name "stats/error-count-of" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/error-count-of key) -> integer-or-string"
  :summary "Return the lifetime count for one error-key. 0 if that error has never escaped. Accepts a symbol or a string."
  :explanation "The sharp accessor version of stats/error-counts, mirroring stats/verb-calls-of. Give it an error-key — either a symbol like 'unbound-symbol or the string \"unbound-symbol\" — and it returns how many times that failure has escaped a program, ever. Returns 0 (not null) for a key that has never fired. BigInt-safe: returns a string if the count exceeds 2^53-1."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/error-count-of \"unbound-symbol\")"
     :note "Times an unbound symbol has escaped, ever.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(> (stats/error-count-of \"division-by-zero\") 0)"
     :note "'Has this failure ever happened here?' predicate.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(map (lambda (row) (list (car row) (stats/error-count-of (car row))))\n     (stats/error-counts 5))"
     :note "Cross-read: confirm the top-5 counts agree with the sharp accessor."))
  :caveats ("Zero for errors that never escaped — not null. Distinguishes 'known error shape, never fired' from 'unknown'." "Key must match the normalizer's output; pass a key from stats/error-counts, not a raw message.")
  :drawbacks ("One key at a time — for the ranking use stats/error-counts.")
  :usecases ("One-error health check." "Regression guard: assert a fixed error stays at its old count." "Per-key reporting.")
  :related ("stats/error-counts" "stats/error-summary" "stats/verb-calls-of")
  :learn
    (:concept "Sharp read of one failure's lifetime count."
     :prerequisites ("Understand the error-key normalizer.")
     :progression "Read a key before and after a change to prove you didn't introduce that failure.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You fixed a bug; you want to assert that its error-key stops climbing."
     :eng-program "(define (regressed? key baseline)\n  (let ((n (stats/error-count-of key)))\n    (cond ((string? n) 'very-frequent)\n          ((> n baseline) 'regressed)\n          (else 'held))))\n(regressed? \"unbound-symbol\" 0)"
     :math-program ";; One failure mode's SHARE of all escaped errors: count(key) / total-errors. A real relative-frequency in [0,1] — how much of the wrong-sink this specific error accounts for, read live.\n(let* ((c (stats/error-count-of \"unbound-symbol\"))\n       (total (cadr (assoc :total-errors (stats/error-summary)))))\n  (if (> total 0)\n      (list :key \"unbound-symbol\" :count c :share (exact->inexact (/ c total)))\n      (list :key \"unbound-symbol\" :count c :share 0)))"
     :cs-program "(define (nonneg-or-bigint? key)\n  (let ((n (stats/error-count-of key)))\n    (or (and (integer? n) (>= n 0))\n        (string? n))))\n(nonneg-or-bigint? \"unbound-symbol\")"
     :explanation "eng: regression guard on a fixed error. math: single-key share of all escaped errors (relative frequency in [0,1]), computed live. cs: type-witness (int-or-string, always ≥ 0)."
     :meta "Sibling: stats/error-counts gives you the ranking; this gives you one key.")
    (:eng "One-error regression predicate."
     :math "stats/error-count-of : Key → ℕ ∪ String."
     :cs   "Sharp read into the wrong-sink."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Sharp accessor into a counter store, keyed by a failure shape."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "error count" :code "(stats/error-count-of \"unbound-symbol\")")
       (:domain "verb count"  :code "(stats/verb-calls-of 'car)")
       (:domain "hash lookup" :code "(assoc :doc (help 'car))"))
     :explanation "One-key read is a universal shape; here the key is an error."
     :meta "Sharp read discipline, applied to failures.")
    (:eng "Read one failure's count, cheaply."
     :math "Function evaluation at one error-key."
     :cs   "O(1) counter read."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Non-negative; 0 for an unseen key; monotone non-decreasing in real time."
     :eng-program "(define (unseen-is-zero? key)\n  (let ((n (stats/error-count-of key)))\n    (or (string? n) (>= n 0))))\n(unseen-is-zero? \"a-key-that-never-fired\")"
     :math-program ";; A real ordering invariant proved live: any single error's count is bounded — non-negative AND no greater than total-errors, so 0 <= count(key) <= total-errors. Verify both bounds at once.\n(let ((c (stats/error-count-of \"unbound-symbol\"))\n      (total (cadr (assoc :total-errors (stats/error-summary)))))\n  (and (>= c 0) (<= c total)))"
     :cs-program "(define (zero-for-unknown?)\n  (integer? (stats/error-count-of \"definitely-not-a-real-error-key-xyz\")))\n(zero-for-unknown?)"
     :invariant "∀ key. stats/error-count-of(key) ≥ 0 ∧ = 0 when never seen ∧ monotone non-decreasing."
     :meta "No negative counts; unseen is a clean zero, not an error.")
    (:eng "Counter is monotonic; unseen is zero."
     :math "Range: ℕ ∪ String (BigInt overflow)."
     :cs   "Total function; no missing-key exception."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/error-count-of returns one error-key's lifetime count."
     :eng-composition "(define (health-of keys)\n  ;; A tiny health panel: each watched error and its count.\n  (map (lambda (k) (list k (stats/error-count-of k))) keys))\n(health-of '(\"unbound-symbol\" \"division-by-zero\" \"wrong-arity\"))"
     :math-composition ";; A failure-health VECTOR: count several error keys at once, giving a fixed-length profile of the wrong-sink. A real componentwise projection over telemetry — the runtime's failure fingerprint.\n(map (lambda (k) (list k (stats/error-count-of k)))\n     (list \"unbound-symbol\" \"not-a-procedure\" \"division-by-zero\" \"wrong-arity\"))"
     :cs-composition "(define (any-fired? keys)\n  ;; Guard: has ANY watched failure escaped this build?\n  (let loop ((ks keys))\n    (cond ((null? ks) #f)\n          ((let ((n (stats/error-count-of (car ks))))\n             (or (string? n) (> n 0))) #t)\n          (else (loop (cdr ks))))))\n(any-fired? '(\"frozen-sandbox\" \"program-too-big\"))"
     :emergent "A HEALTH-VECTOR + any-fired guard emerges from stats/error-count-of + a watch-list: the sink becomes a targeted regression alarm."
     :meta "Same slot as a Prometheus per-error counter query — but composed inside the language.")
    (:eng "Watch specific failures; alarm on any."
     :math "Projection over an error-key set."
     :cs   "Targeted regression guard.")) )

(verb
  :name "stats/error-summary" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/error-summary) -> alist"
  :summary "Return a top-level summary of the error census: distinct-errors, total-errors, real, provisional, novel-this-session, errors-this-session, stats-slat."
  :explanation "The one-verb dashboard for the wrong-sink — the error mirror of stats/summary. Alist keys: :distinct-errors (how many error-keys are tracked), :total-errors (lifetime sum of all escaped errors), :real and :provisional (how many keys have promoted vs not), :novel-this-session (keys first-ever-seen this process), :errors-this-session (escaped errors since startup), :stats-slat (the path where the census persists)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/error-summary)"
     :note "The whole wrong-sink at a glance.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(cadr (assoc :real (stats/error-summary)))"
     :note "How many failure shapes have recurred enough to be 'real'.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(let* ((s (stats/error-summary))\n       (distinct (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors s)))\n       (total    (cadr (assoc :total-errors s))))\n  (if (> distinct 0) (/ total distinct) 0))"
     :note "Mean recurrence per error-key — how repetitive the failures are."))
  :caveats ("errors-this-session and novel-this-session are CURRENT session only; distinct/total/real/provisional are lifetime.")
  :drawbacks ("Not per-key; for that use stats/error-counts.")
  :usecases ("REPL banner: 'how healthy has this session been?'" "One-line failure dashboard." "Deciding whether a build introduced new failure shapes.")
  :related ("stats/error-counts" "stats/error-count-of" "stats/error-novel" "stats/summary")
  :learn
    (:concept "Summarize the entire wrong-sink in one call."
     :prerequisites ("Understand alists." "Understand provisional vs real.")
     :progression "Print stats/error-summary at REPL open next to stats/summary for a full self-census banner.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "You want a health line: 'did anything new break this session?'"
     :eng-program "(define (health-line)\n  (let ((s (stats/error-summary)))\n    (list ':novel   (cadr (assoc :novel-this-session s))\n          ':session (cadr (assoc :errors-this-session s))\n          ':real    (cadr (assoc :real s)))))\n(health-line)"
     :math-program ";; Mean recurrence per distinct error: total-errors / distinct-errors. A real average — how many times, on average, each unique failure mode has fired. Near 1 means one-offs; high means repeated pain.\n(let* ((s (stats/error-summary))\n       (total (cadr (assoc :total-errors s)))\n       (distinct (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors s))))\n  (if (> distinct 0) (exact->inexact (/ total distinct)) 0))"
     :cs-program "(define (has-all-keys?)\n  (let ((s (stats/error-summary)))\n    (and (assoc :distinct-errors s)\n         (assoc :total-errors s)\n         (assoc :real s)\n         (assoc :provisional s)\n         (assoc :novel-this-session s)\n         (assoc :errors-this-session s)\n         (assoc :stats-slat s))))\n(has-all-keys?)"
     :explanation "eng: session health line. math: mean recurrence per distinct error (total-errors / distinct-errors), derived live. cs: schema-completeness check."
     :meta "One-verb wrong-sink dashboard.")
    (:eng "Session-level failure observability."
     :math "Summary is a fold over per-key error counters."
     :cs   "One call, the whole census."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Roll a set of counters into a single summary — here, of failures."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "error summary" :code "(stats/error-summary)")
       (:domain "usage summary" :code "(stats/summary)")
       (:domain "list length"   :code "(length '(a b c))"))
     :explanation "Summarization is universal: how much of X do we have? Here X is wrongs."
     :meta "The wrong-sink summarizes the same way the right-census does.")
    (:eng "Report failures at the top of the loop."
     :math "Aggregation as a value."
     :cs   "Rollups are cheap, even for errors."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "real + provisional = distinct-errors; stats-slat equals (stats/path)."
     :eng-program "(define (cross-check)\n  (let* ((s (stats/error-summary))\n         (real (cadr (assoc :real s)))\n         (prov (cadr (assoc :provisional s)))\n         (dist (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors s))))\n    (list ':partition-holds (= (+ real prov) dist)\n          ':paths-match (equal? (cadr (assoc :stats-slat s)) (stats/path)))))\n(cross-check)"
     :math-program ";; A real partition identity proved live: every distinct error is EITHER real OR provisional, never both, never neither — so real + provisional = distinct-errors. Verify the two-state partition sums exactly.\n(let ((s (stats/error-summary)))\n  (= (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors s))\n     (+ (cadr (assoc :real s)) (cadr (assoc :provisional s)))))"
     :cs-program "(define (partition-holds?)\n  (let ((s (stats/error-summary)))\n    (= (+ (cadr (assoc :real s)) (cadr (assoc :provisional s)))\n       (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors s)))))\n(partition-holds?)"
     :invariant "real + provisional = distinct-errors ∧ summary[:stats-slat] = stats/path()."
     :meta "Two-state promotion partitions the key set exactly.")
    (:eng "Summary is honest and self-consistent."
     :math "Partition identity across the key states."
     :cs   "Every key is provisional xor real."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/error-summary returns a top-level wrong-sink dashboard."
     :eng-composition "(define (self-census)\n  ;; The full mirror: what ran, and what broke.\n  (list ':usage  (stats/summary)\n        ':errors (stats/error-summary)))\n(self-census)"
     :math-composition ";; Promotion ratio of the wrong-sink: real / distinct — the fraction of failure modes that have recurred often enough to graduate from provisional to real (confirmed patterns). A real maturity metric in [0,1].\n(let* ((s (stats/error-summary))\n       (real (cadr (assoc :real s)))\n       (distinct (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors s))))\n  (if (> distinct 0) (exact->inexact (/ real distinct)) 0))"
     :cs-composition "(define (clean-session?)\n  ;; A session is clean if nothing new broke.\n  (= 0 (cadr (assoc :novel-this-session (stats/error-summary)))))\n(clean-session?)"
     :emergent "A TWO-SIDED SELF-CENSUS + clean-session predicate emerges from stats/summary + stats/error-summary: the console reports what worked and what broke in one breath, and can gate on 'nothing new broke'."
     :meta "Same slot as an error-rate SLO next to a throughput metric — but composed inside the language.")
    (:eng "One dashboard for rights, one for wrongs."
     :math "Projection combinators over two alists."
     :cs   "The self-census is a function.")) )

(verb
  :name "stats/error-novel" :std "Motoi"
  :library "introspection"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(stats/error-novel) -> alist"
  :summary "Return the error-keys first-ever-seen in THIS session — the mint side of match-or-mint — as an alist of (:key count status)."
  :explanation "Zero-arg. Returns only the error-keys that were minted (seen for the very first time, ever) during the current process — not keys carried in from disk. This is how the console notices 'a wrong I have never seen before just happened.' Each row is (:error-key count status). In a batch `run` an escaped error ends the program, so this is most useful in a long-lived REPL or the copilot loop, where a new failure can be noticed and reasoned about while the session continues."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(stats/error-novel)"
     :note "The brand-new failure shapes from this session.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(null? (stats/error-novel))"
     :note "'Has anything unprecedented broken this session?' — #t means no.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(map car (stats/error-novel))"
     :note "Just the new error-keys — candidates to mint a provisional card for."))
  :caveats ("Session-scoped: resets every process. A key loaded from ~/.motoi/stats.slat is NOT novel even if this is the first time you queried it." "guard-handled errors are never novel — they never entered the census.")
  :drawbacks ("Empty in a batch run that ends on its first escaped error — you can't read it after the program halts. Built for live sessions.")
  :usecases ("Notice a never-before-seen failure the moment it happens." "Feed the mint side of match-or-mint: new key → provisional card candidate." "Gate a session on 'nothing unprecedented broke'.")
  :related ("stats/error-summary" "stats/error-counts" "stats/error-count-of")
  :learn
    (:concept "The census distinguishes a recurring wrong from an unprecedented one."
     :prerequisites ("Understand match-or-mint." "Understand session vs lifetime scope.")
     :progression "In a REPL, cause a fresh error, then read (stats/error-novel) to see it minted.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "In a live session you want to react the instant a never-seen failure appears."
     :eng-program "(define (report-novelty)\n  (let ((novel (stats/error-novel)))\n    (if (null? novel)\n        'nothing-new\n        (map car novel))))\n(report-novelty)"
     :math-program ";; Cardinality of the novel set: |{k : k minted this session}| — how many distinct never-before-seen failure modes appeared, computed live. Zero in a clean session; each mint increments it.\n(length (stats/error-novel))"
     :cs-program "(define (novel-rows-well-formed?)\n  (let loop ((rs (stats/error-novel)))\n    (cond ((null? rs) #t)\n          ((and (pair? (car rs)) (memq (caddr (car rs)) '(provisional real)))\n           (loop (cdr rs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(novel-rows-well-formed?)"
     :explanation "eng: novelty reactor. math: cardinality of the novel set — |{k minted this session}|, a live count in ℕ. cs: row-shape check."
     :meta "Sibling: stats/error-summary counts novelty; this names it.")
    (:eng "React to unprecedented failures live."
     :math "The minted-this-session subset of the key space."
     :cs   "Session-scoped set read."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Project the 'new this session' subset out of a persistent store."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "novel errors"  :code "(stats/error-novel)")
       (:domain "novel count"   :code "(length (stats/error-novel))")
       (:domain "set diff"      :code "(filter (lambda (x) (not (memv x '(1 2)))) '(1 2 3))"))
     :explanation "Novelty is a set-difference shape: what's here now that wasn't before."
     :meta "Minting is the error census's distinctive move.")
    (:eng "New-this-session is a first-class query."
     :math "Set difference: session-keys minus disk-keys."
     :cs   "The mint boundary is observable."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Every novel key is a subset of the full census; each appears with a valid status."
     :eng-program "(define (novel-subset-of-all?)\n  (let ((novel-keys (map car (stats/error-novel)))\n        (all-keys   (map car (stats/error-counts 100000))))\n    (let loop ((ks novel-keys))\n      (cond ((null? ks) #t)\n            ((member (car ks) all-keys) (loop (cdr ks)))\n            (else #f)))))\n(novel-subset-of-all?)"
     :math-program ";; Subset-cardinality bound: |novel| ≤ distinct-errors. Because every minted key is also counted, the novel set is a subset of the census, so its size can never exceed the census's distinct count. A checkable state-consistency invariant.\n(<= (length (stats/error-novel))\n    (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors (stats/error-summary))))"
     :cs-program "(define (novel-count-le-distinct?)\n  (<= (length (stats/error-novel))\n      (cadr (assoc :distinct-errors (stats/error-summary)))))\n(novel-count-le-distinct?)"
     :invariant "novel ⊆ census ∧ |novel| ≤ distinct-errors ∧ every novel status ∈ {provisional, real}."
     :meta "Minting adds to the census; it never produces a key outside it.")
    (:eng "Novel keys are always real census entries."
     :math "Subset invariant over runtime key sets."
     :cs   "|novel| ≤ |distinct|."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "stats/error-novel returns the keys minted this session."
     :eng-composition "(define (on-novel handler)\n  ;; Call handler with each new error-key; the copilot's mint hook.\n  (for-each (lambda (row) (handler (car row))) (stats/error-novel)))\n(on-novel (lambda (k) (display \"new wrong: \") (display k) (newline)))"
     :math-composition ";; Join the novel key set against the count function: {(k, count(k)) : k ∈ novel} — pairs each never-before-seen failure with how many times it has fired this session. A relation built by composing error-novel with error-count-of. Empty in a clean session.\n(map (lambda (k) (list k (stats/error-count-of k)))\n     (map car (stats/error-novel)))"
     :cs-composition "(define (session-clean?)\n  ;; A session is clean when nothing unprecedented broke.\n  (null? (stats/error-novel)))\n(session-clean?)"
     :emergent "A MINT-EVENT stream + clean-session gate emerges from stats/error-novel + a handler: the moment an unprecedented wrong appears, the copilot can abduce a cause and draft a provisional card — mechanism 4 of the closed loop, live."
     :meta "Same slot as a 'first-seen exception' alert in an error tracker — but composed inside the language, feeding the wrong-sink.")
    (:eng "Unprecedented failures become events to act on."
     :math "Event stream over the minted subset."
     :cs   "The mint boundary drives card creation.")) )

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; End of chapter 00b.
